+# Shared Local Runtime Owner And Lease Protocol
+
+Task: `TASK-260825-imfmgz`
+Story: `STORY-260825-1r7z9o`
+Epic: `EPIC-260713-259g2s`
+Supersedes for shared mode only: `.research/260817_pi-local-model-launch-contract.md` sections 7, 9, 10
+Status: implementation-ready specification
+Revision: 9 — closes review `RUN-260825-86b7d5` finding F1. See section 16 for
+the closure map. Revision 9, like revisions 6, 7 and 8 before it, changes **no
+accepted or refused frame**: the decoder boundary, the refusal table, the field
+set and `protocol_version = 6` are exactly as revision 6 left them, and this time
+that is *measured* rather than argued — revision 8's wiring is retained under a
+name and required to agree with revision 9's on all 417 frames in verdict, reason
+and named member (§12.3.1, probe P23.F). Section 9's bump rule read the other way
+is why nothing moves: the set of frames the launcher admits did not change, so
+there is no client a bump would protect.
+
+**What revision 9 fixes is the evidence about the evidence.** Revision 8's proof
+rested on a table of eighteen mutants, each required to be killed by the
+generated corpus. Review `RUN-260825-86b7d5` found that one of them was not the
+mutant its row described: `unknown_by_wire_form` was wired through a predicate
+that discarded the member's wire spelling, so it refused *every* member of every
+frame. It was killed by 48 witnesses, and all 48 were ordinary **valid** frames.
+Its `blind` label was a boolean in a table that nothing executed.
+
+The rule that follows generalises past this document: **a mutation harness's
+controls are gates, and the same evidence rule applies to them.** "The mutant was
+killed" is a positive result about the harness; nothing in revision 8 would have
+failed if a mutant were the wrong mutant. §12.3.1 makes three obligations
+normative — every mutant must still admit the plain valid frame, must disagree
+with the oracle only on the side its row declares, and must have its blindness
+**measured** against a named inventory of hand-written rows, failing on an
+over-claim and an under-claim alike — and adds `reject_all_probe`, the review's
+defect reproduced on purpose so all three can be shown reddening.
+
+Rule 3 immediately corrected the document. Revision 8 claimed seven mutants blind
+to every hand-written row; measurement says **four**. The two mutants review
+`RUN-260825-c71188` inspired are not blind to that review's own rows — those rows
+are what caught them — and `dup_only_when_separated` is caught by
+`dup_same_exec_plan_digest`, the one same-value duplicate row whose appended
+repeat lands *adjacent* to its original because `exec_plan_digest` is the last
+member in the frame's field order. None of that was reasoned out; the harness
+reported it on its first run.
+
+Five reviews have now found the same shape of defect, each one class further out:
+
+| Review | What it defeated |
+| --- | --- |
+| `RUN-260825-969723` | the version gate existed only in prose; the launcher parsed the field and never compared it |
+| `RUN-260825-a8a4ef` | five equality comparisons closed nothing — the decode had already discarded the evidence |
+| `RUN-260825-9d5cff` | the decoder was right and the **proof** sampled it: one duplicate value pair, one unknown name |
+| `RUN-260825-c71188` | still sampled, on two dimensions nobody had named — every revision-7 duplicate row had arity exactly **two**, and every revision-7 unknown name was at most **19 bytes** |
+| `RUN-260825-86b7d5` | the corpus and the oracle were sound, and one **mutant** was not the mutant its row described: a reject-all fake credited with a kill, with a blindness label nothing executed |
+
+Revision 7 answered the third review by adding rows. The fourth review then built
+`count == 2` and `length <= 32` and kept every one of those rows green. Adding
+two more rows loses again, one dimension further out, and this document has now
+spent three revisions demonstrating that. **The rows are not the problem: naming
+the admitted class in advance is.** Revision 8 stops doing it.
+
+Two changes follow, and they are halves of one claim:
+
+- **The rule is restated constructively.** §6.2 B12 step 4 no longer reads as
+  three clauses to be satisfied. It reads as one decision — the decoded member
+  multiset must **equal** the closed allowed set with multiplicity one — with the
+  three clauses demoted to a *classification of the difference*, which is what
+  they always were and which is why they were narrowable one at a time. A
+  decision that is one equality against a compiled constant has one bound to
+  prove, and normatively **must not be expressed as a predicate over any quantity
+  derived from the frame** — not an occurrence count, not a name length, not a
+  position, not the bytes of a key.
+- **The proof is a differential against an independent oracle**, not a table of
+  frames and expected verdicts (§12.2.30c, probe P23). A generated corpus sweeps
+  the decision's degrees of freedom; the expected verdict is *computed* by a
+  separate decision procedure rather than written next to each frame; and every
+  mutant must be killed **by the corpus**, with the harness reporting which
+  generated frame killed it and failing on a mutant nothing kills. A narrowed
+  gate is then caught because it **disagrees with the oracle**, not because
+  somebody predicted the class it admits.
+
+What that does not buy is stated in §12.2.30c rather than left to the next
+review: a generated corpus is still a subset of an infinite space, and a
+narrowing whose admitted class is disjoint from every dimension swept survives
+P23 exactly as `count == 2` survived revision 7. The corpus bounds the sample.
+What bounds the space is the structural obligation — the decision must be an
+equality against a constant set, and §12.4 requires the production launcher to
+record the multiset it decided on. Neither half is evidence alone.
+
+Revision 7 — closed review `RUN-260825-9d5cff` finding F1. It changed no
+specified behaviour either; what it changed was the evidence, and its reason is
+worth keeping because it is a third distinct way to be wrong about the same gate.
+
+Revision 5 was wrong about *where* closure lives — it asked five comparisons to
+close a field set. Revision 6 moved closure to the decoder and was right about
+where it lives, but its proof named one duplicate value pair and one unknown
+member name, and a rule stated as a class cannot be proved by a sample. The
+sixth review built two decoders that keep every revision-6 row green while
+admitting frames this specification refuses: one that refuses a duplicate only
+when the two values **differ**, which admits `protocol_version` written twice
+with the same valid value; and one that refuses only the single unknown name the
+row happened to use, which admits every other unknown name. Neither is caught by
+a delete-only mutant, because deleting a clause proves the clause exists and
+says nothing about the class it covers.
+
+Revision 7 therefore proves the two dimensions rather than sampling them. The
+allowed set is **closed and finite**, so "exactly once" is proved exhaustively:
+all five members, each duplicated with its own valid value, one row each. The
+unknown-name space is **infinite**, so it is proved by named near-miss classes —
+an unrelated name, a case variant of an allowed name, and an allowed name used
+as a prefix — each paired with a narrowed mutant that only that class reddens.
+Five narrowed mutants join the deletions, and section 12.2.30c records what each
+one leaves green.
+
+One measured fact came out of building that evidence and is now normative in B12
+step 4, because it would have made the case-variant row worthless: Go's
+`encoding/json` matches an object key to a struct field by exact tag first and by
+**case-folded name** second. `"Schema"` is therefore not discarded the way
+`"future_extension"` is — it is absorbed into `schema` and overwrites it. A case
+variant carrying a *wrong* value is caught by the equality comparison and proves
+nothing about the unknown-member clause; only one carrying the *right* value
+reaches the comparisons with everything equal, leaving the shape gate as the only
+thing between it and `execve`. That is the same trap as revision 6's single
+duplicate order, one level down: **an attack that a different gate happens to
+stop is not evidence for the gate under test.**
+
+Revision 6 — closed review `RUN-260825-a8a4ef` finding F1. Revision 6 is narrower
+still than revision 5, and for the same reason one step further in: it changes no
+ownership, recovery or lease semantics, and it exists because revision 5 stated a
+rule that its own mechanism could not enforce.
+
+Revision 5 declared the authorization frame's field set **closed** at five
+members and enforced that closure with five equality comparisons. The fifth
+review minted two frames — five valid fields plus an unknown sixth member, and
+`protocol_version = 999` followed by the valid `protocol_version`, in one object
+— and both reached `execve` while all five comparisons stayed green. They stayed
+green because they were all *true*: an ordinary JSON decode discards unknown
+members and keeps the last of duplicate keys, so the launcher compared exactly
+the five values it expected and never learned that the frame had carried
+anything else.
+
+The lesson is narrow and worth stating plainly, because it is the third time
+this task has met the same shape from a different side: **a comparison cannot
+close a field set.** A comparison answers *is this value the one I expect*. It
+cannot answer *were these the only bytes the frame carried*, and only the
+decoder is in a position to answer that. Revision 6 therefore moves the closure
+from the comparisons to the decoder boundary (B12 step 4): one JSON object,
+each of the five allowed keys exactly once, nothing after it — refused as a
+`protocol_violation` before any value is compared and long before `execve`.
+
+**B12's step numbering changed in revision 6.** The shape gate is inserted as
+step 4, so the field comparisons are now step 5 and `execve` is step 6. Steps 1
+to 3 are untouched. Any text elsewhere that places the comparison table at
+"step 4" is stale, not a second convention.
+
+Revision 4's own summary still holds and is why the ownership sections are
+untouched here: it answered its three blocking findings by removing three things
+the protocol was asking a process to *infer*: the launcher no longer has to be
+told what to run, because it recomposes it; the client no longer decides
+anything about a broker at its own deadline, because it cannot see whether that
+broker is starting or serving; and the broker no longer owns anything before it
+is identifiable, because it publishes its owner record as the first action after
+winning the election.
+
+**Broker step numbering changed in revision 4.** Two steps were inserted and one
+was hoisted, so every `B<n>` label moved. Section 6.0 is the exact
+revision-3 → revision-4 mapping; any text elsewhere that still reads like
+revision 3's numbering is a defect, not a second convention.
