+## 1. Decision summary
+
+agents-infra shall add an opt-in **shared runtime mode** in which one
+agents-infra-owned broker process owns exactly one managed local model runtime
+and hands verified loopback endpoints to many independent Pi clients under
+process-bound leases.
+
+1. Sharing is per profile and opt-in through `[agents.pi.profiles."NAME".runtime.sharing]`.
+   Absence keeps today's exclusive single-client behavior byte-for-byte, so no
+   existing project configuration changes meaning.
+2. The broker is a detached session leader with no controlling terminal, and it
+   survives every client. Stated exactly, because revision 2 overstated it: the
+   broker is forked by whichever client happened to start it and remains that
+   client's OS **child** until that client exits, at which point it reparents to
+   the init process. What makes it terminal-independent is `setsid`, not
+   reparenting — its session, process group, controlling terminal and standard
+   descriptors are its own from birth, so killing the starter's terminal, shell,
+   spawn-runner, process group or session has no effect on it. Keeping it a real
+   child is deliberate: it is how the starter learns, immediately, whether its
+   broker lost the startup election or died.
+3. The broker is the **direct parent** of the runtime process. The cycle-8
+   invariant "the endpoint is backed by a live direct child we spawned" is
+   preserved; it moves from the client to the broker and reaches clients through
+   an attested IPC channel.
+3a. **Ownership begins with identity, not before it.** After winning the
+   election the broker does exactly two things before anything else: it reads the
+   predecessor's record (B3) and publishes its own elected-owner record (B4). Only
+   then does it compose its profile, reclaim a predecessor's runtime, or preflight
+   the port. Revision 3 published only after all three, which left a valid broker
+   holding the lock with no record for as long as reclaiming a 27B runtime takes,
+   and then called that state impossible. It is not impossible; revision 4 narrows
+   it to one atomic write with no blocking call inside it and, where it remains
+   observable, reports it honestly as `starting-unverified` rather than as
+   `absent` (section 3.1, section 10.2, probe P16). B4 carries the predecessor's
+   unreclaimed runtime block forward, because a single-file record that forgot it
+   would erase the only name of a runtime that may still be alive (probe P17).
+3b. **Single-flight is a kernel fact the broker establishes about itself.** A
+   broker's first *side effect* is `flock(broker.lock, LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB)` on a
+   descriptor it opened in its own process; the only thing preceding it is a
+   `getsid` self-check that touches nothing. Losing that race is the entire
+   content of "another broker owns this runtime": the loser exits before it reads
+   or writes the record, before reclamation, port preflight, runtime creation and
+   bind, so it has no side effect to undo. No process ever has to infer whether
+   some other open file description holds the lock.
+3c. **A runtime cannot exist before the record that identifies it.** The broker
+   creates the runtime process as an *unauthorized launcher* — its own
+   executable, blocked on an authorization pipe — publishes the durable identity
+   record naming that pid, and only then authorizes the launcher to `execve` the
+   real runtime in place. `execve` preserves pid, pgid and process start time, so
+   the record written before the runtime existed still identifies it afterwards.
+   A broker killed at any instant therefore leaves either no runtime or a
+   recorded one, never a live unrecorded one. Revision 4 extends the same rule
+   backwards over the *predecessor's* runtime: because the owner record is now
+   published before reclamation runs, that publication carries the predecessor's
+   unreclaimed runtime block forward verbatim, so the successor's own record
+   never stops naming a runtime that is still alive (probe P17).
+3d. **The launcher is told whether to run, never what to run.** The
+   authorization frame carries no executable, no argv, no timeout and no cwd.
+   The launcher is the broker's own binary, so it re-runs the same full profile
+   composition from the same project directory and derives all four itself; the
+   recomputed `runtime_key` must equal the one on its command line. A hash cannot
+   be inverted, which is exactly why revision 3's `--runtime-key`-only contract
+   was not implementable (probe P18). The frame's five members are closed **at
+   the decoder** — one object, each key exactly once, nothing after it — because
+   a comparison can only say that a value is the expected one and can never say
+   that no other value arrived (B12 step 4, probes P22 and P23).
+3e. **A client's acquisition deadline is not shutdown authority.** A client that
+   reaches its deadline signals nothing. It cannot see whether the broker it
+   forked is still starting or is already serving another RUN's lease — those two
+   states present the identical `wait4(WNOHANG)` answer — so it reports what it
+   observed and refuses (probe P19).
+4. A lease is an open `AF_UNIX` connection. Kernel connection teardown is the
+   authoritative release signal, so a client that is `SIGKILL`ed releases its
+   lease exactly and immediately. Heartbeats are observability and wedge
+   detection, never a revocation authority.
+5. Attaching to an already-running listener is permitted **only** through the
+   broker after a full identity attestation chain. Every other occupied listener
+   is refused exactly as today. Sharing never weakens arbitrary-listener refusal;
+   it narrows the single case where ownership is provable.
+6. Operator surfaces are the new top-level `agents-infra runtime status` and
+   `agents-infra runtime stop`. `agents-infra pi --print-config` stays
+   non-connecting.
+7. The runtime is terminated and reaped when the final lease is released, when
+   the operator stops it, or when the broker exits for any recoverable reason.
+   `linger_seconds = 0` is the acceptance-proof configuration.
+8. Every decision that reads "is the recorded process alive" requires the
+   process to exist **and not be a zombie**. `kern.proc.pid` answers
+   successfully for an exited, unreaped process; treating that as liveness would
+   let one unreaped corpse block every future runtime start (section 2, probe
+   P15).
+
+Traceability: this section implements `STORY-260825-1r7z9o` scope clauses
+"terminal-independent broker", "acquire leases automatically", "reuse one
+verified loopback model server", "release leases on tracked child termination",
+"reject arbitrary or identity-mismatched listeners", and "stop/reap the runtime
+only after the final tracked lease expires or an operator explicitly stops it".
+
+## 2. Verified platform facts
+
+Every mechanism below was probed on the target host (darwin 25.5.0, arm64,
+Go 1.25.5, `golang.org/x/sys v0.30.0`) before being specified. Probe source and
+raw output: `.temp/TASK-260825-imfmgz/probe`.
+
+| Mechanism | Call | Observed result |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Process uid + start time | `unix.SysctlKinfoProc("kern.proc.pid", pid)` | `uid=501 start=1787658067.968582` for self and for a spawned child |
+| Process exec path + argv | `unix.SysctlRaw("kern.procargs2", pid)` | `/bin/sleep` with argv `["/bin/sleep" "97"]` |
+| Dead **and reaped** pid | same | `input/output error` — a fully gone pid is decidable, not silently "ok" |
+| Dead **unreaped** pid (zombie) | same | **succeeds** — see probe P15; `kern.proc.pid` alone is not a liveness test |
+| Foreign-uid pid | same | `invalid argument` — argv of another user is unreadable |
+| UDS peer uid | `unix.GetsockoptXucred(fd, unix.SOL_LOCAL, unix.LOCAL_PEERCRED)` | `uid=501`, no error |
+| UDS peer pid | `unix.GetsockoptInt(fd, unix.SOL_LOCAL, unix.LOCAL_PEERPID)` | peer pid, no error |
+| `sun_path` limit | `net.Listen("unix", <189 byte path>)` | `bind: invalid argument` |
+| `flock` handoff | flock, inherit FD through `fork`+`exec`, close starter copy | second `LOCK_EX\|LOCK_NB` refused with `EWOULDBLOCK`; released on holder death |
+
+Two consequences are load-bearing:
+
+- A full-key socket path does **not** fit. `os.UserCacheDir()` is
+  `/Users/alexis/Library/Caches` (28 bytes); `28 + len("/agents-infra/pi/") + 64
+  + 1 + 64 + len("/broker.sock") = 186` bytes against a 103-byte usable
+  `sun_path`. Section 5.2 therefore uses a truncated rendezvous address that is
+  never an identity.
+- `flock` on an open file description survives `fork`+`exec` and is released
+  only when every descriptor referring to that description is closed. Section 6
+  uses this for a starter→broker lock handoff with no window in which the lock
+  is unheld.
+
+Foreign-uid `kern.procargs2` returning `EINVAL` is a **refusal input**, not an
+absence: a runtime owned by another uid can never be attested, so it can never
+be attached to.
+
+**Revision 2 probes.** The first independent review demonstrated that two
+revision-1 mechanisms were not implementable as written. Every mechanism that
+replaced them was probed on the same host before being specified. Source:
+`.temp/TASK-260825-imfmgz/probe-rev2/`; raw output:
+`TASK-260825-imfmgz_probe-rev2-results.log`.
+
+| Probe | Question | Observed result |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| P1 † | Can a process prove it holds a lock on an inherited descriptor? | Re-locking the same fd returns `nil` whether held or not — **the revision-1 gate was inert**. A *second* descriptor returns `EWOULDBLOCK` when held and acquires when unheld, and closing the probing fd leaves the real lock intact |
+| P2 † | Does the two-part gate work across a real `fork`+`exec` handoff? | Held-and-correct fd ⇒ `OK`; unlocked fd, fd pointing at a different file, and no inherited fd ⇒ `broker_lock_not_inherited`. All four cases distinguished |
+| P3 | Can a second broker bind the rendezvous path? | `EADDRINUSE` against a live listener **and** against a stale socket inode — bind never silently takes over |
+| P4 | Is "no broker yet" distinguishable from "stale inode"? | No inode ⇒ `ENOENT`; inode present, nobody accepting ⇒ `ECONNREFUSED`; live ⇒ connects |
+| P5 | Does gate 3 refuse a stale broker after a rename-over upgrade? | **No** — the recorded path resolves to the new inode and gate 3 passes. The start-time-recorded inode (gate 3b) does refuse it |
+| P6 | Is a `Setsid` child reparented to init immediately? | **No** — `ppid` is still the starter's pid; the child is its own session leader. `wait4(WNOHANG)` reaps it and reports its exit status |
+| P7 ‡ | Does the revision-1 startup algorithm starve a concurrent second client? | **Yes** — `broker_start_timeout` after the full deadline while the broker was connectable. The revised loop attaches as soon as the peer binds, elects exactly one starter out of eight racers, and promotes a waiter when the incumbent dies |
+
+† P1 and P2 probed the inherited-lock gate revision 2 specified. That gate is
+**withdrawn** in revision 3 — the second review showed it passes on a peer's lock
+during startup (F2). The probes remain here because they record why the
+mechanism could not be repaired: P1 shows the same-descriptor check is inert, and
+P2's four distinguished cases are still not enough, since none of them is the
+attack shape. Revision 3's answer is P8.
+
+‡ P7's finding — never block on the lock — survives in a stronger form: revision
+3's client never opens `broker.lock` at all.
+
+One further fact emerged from P7 and is load-bearing for section 6: on darwin,
+`close(2)` on a descriptor that another thread is blocked in `flock(LOCK_EX)` on
+**itself blocks** until the `flock` returns. A client that abandons a blocking
+lock acquisition therefore cannot release its descriptor at all; it can only
+exit the process. That is an independent reason the revised algorithm never
+blocks on the lock. Revision 3 removes the question entirely: the client
+never opens `broker.lock` at all.
+
+**Revision 3 probes.** The second independent review (`RUN-260825-ac68af`)
+demonstrated that revision 2's lock gate was bypassable during a peer's startup
+and that its startup ordering could leave a live unrecorded runtime. Revision 3
+replaces both mechanisms, and every replacement was probed on the same host
+before being specified. Source: `.temp/TASK-260825-imfmgz/probe-rev3/`; raw
+output: `TASK-260825-imfmgz_probe-rev3-results.log`.
+
+| Probe | Question | Observed result |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| P8 | Does a broker that acquires `broker.lock` itself give an exact single-flight with no inference? | 8 concurrent candidates ⇒ exactly **1** acquired, 7 exited `election_lost` having bound nothing and created nothing; a fresh candidate after the holder exits wins, so waiter promotion needs no special case |
+| P8.D | The reviewer's exact F2 attack: correct-inode-but-unlocked descriptor 3, a peer holding the lock, the runtime port still free and no rendezvous socket | Refused on the lock alone: `election_lost`, **no** runtime port taken, **no** socket bound, incumbent's lock still held. P8.E is the discriminating control — the same entry point with no incumbent acquires |
+| P9 | Can the broker prove its own detachment from the kernel? | `getsid(0) == getpid()` for a `Setsid` child; **not** equal for a shell-launched or plainly forked one. A hand-typed `agents-infra runtime broker` is refused by a fact about itself |
+| P10 | Does `execve` preserve the identity published before it? | pid, pgid and process **start time byte-identical** across `execve` (`1787662475.779311` before and after); only argv and exec path change. This is what makes publish-before-run implementable |
+| P11 | Can an unauthorized launcher outlive its broker? | Broker's write end closed ⇒ the launcher's read returns EOF ⇒ it exits `unauthorized` **without ever exec'ing**. The write end does not leak through `ExtraFiles`, or EOF would never arrive. A frame naming a foreign pid is refused |
+| P12.A | Does revision 2's ordering actually produce the reviewer's forbidden state? | **Yes** — after `SIGKILL`: runtime **alive**, `broker-state.json` **absent**, `broker.lock` **free**, and reclamation's verdict on the absent record is `no-record` while the runtime is still serving |
+| P12.B/C/D | Is that state reachable under revision 3's ordering? | **No.** Killed before publish ⇒ owner record present and affirmative with no runtime block, launcher exited, no runtime ever existed. Killed between publish and authorize ⇒ launcher exited, record affirmative, verdict `stale-record-process-gone`. Killed after authorize ⇒ orphan identified from the pre-exec record and its group killed |
+| P13 | Does reclamation refuse evidence it cannot verify? | Same pid, different start time ⇒ refused, pid untouched. Pid and start time match but argv is neither admissible shape ⇒ refused, pid untouched. Malformed record ⇒ `unreadable-record`, distinct from `no-record`. Control: the unmutated record does reclaim |
+| P14 | Can an operator stop a live broker whose rendezvous socket is gone? | **Yes**, from the owner record: broker verified by pid + start time + exec path, terminated, lock released, runtime then reclaimed — the socket is never needed. A record with a mismatched broker start time leaves the live broker **untouched** |
+| P15 | Is `kern.proc.pid` a liveness test? | **No** — it succeeds for an exited, unreaped process. `kern.procargs2` refuses it, and `p_stat == SZOMB` distinguishes it. Found because this exact confusion broke P14 in draft |
+
+**Revision 4 probes.** The third independent review (`RUN-260825-969723`)
+demonstrated that revision 3's launcher could not derive what to execute, that a
+client's deadline could terminate a broker already serving a peer, and that a
+valid broker could hold the lock with no record while the specification called
+that state impossible. Every replacement mechanism was probed on the same host
+before being specified. Source: `.temp/TASK-260825-imfmgz/probe-rev4/`; raw
+output: `TASK-260825-imfmgz_probe-rev4-results.log`.
+
+| Probe | Question | Observed result |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| P16.A/B | Is the elected-owner window observable, and is a frozen holder distinguishable from an absent one? | **Yes.** A broker `SIGSTOP`ped between the election and its own publication leaves the lock held and the record absent — the exact F3 state — and the kernel reports `p_stat == SSTOP` for it, so `status` can say `starting-unverified` instead of `absent`. `SIGCONT` makes the record appear with the lock still held, which is what the bounded operator re-poll resolves on. Control: a running owner is `p_stat == SRUN`, so `SSTOP` discriminates |
+| P16.C | Does candidate *reporting* discriminate, or is it a process listing? | A same-uid process holding the same `broker.lock` with an argv that is not a broker invocation for this key is **excluded** from the candidate set |
+| P16.D | Can the candidate set be reduced to one, so `stop --force` could safely signal? | **No.** Two frozen candidates — one holding the lock, one not yet past the election — are indistinguishable on uid, binary inode, argv and `p_stat`. This is why section 10.2 reports and refuses rather than picking one |
+| P17.A ‡ | Does publishing the owner record before reclamation lose the predecessor's runtime? | **Yes**, if the publication drops the runtime block: predecessor runtime **alive**, successor's record present, reclamation verdict `no-runtime-block`. The same forbidden shape as F1 against revision 2, reintroduced by the F3 fix |
+| P17.B/C/D | Does carrying the predecessor's block forward close it? | **Yes.** Same kill instant, verdict `reclaimed`, the runtime pid is killed. Control: with no predecessor block an unrelated same-shape process is left untouched. A malformed record is fatal **before** the successor publishes, so a failed read can never destroy a predecessor's block |
