+## 3. States and transitions
+
+### 3.1 Broker states
+
+```
+                 (starter forks)
+    absent ─────────────────────────► starting
+      ▲                                  │ runtime spawned, ready, attested
+      │                                  ▼
+      │   last lease released      ┌───────────┐   acquire/release
+      ├────────(linger = 0)────────┤  serving  │◄──────────────────┐
+      │                            └───────────┘                   │
+      │                                  │ last lease, linger > 0  │
+      │                                  ▼                         │
+      │                            ┌───────────┐   acquire         │
+      ├──────(linger expires)──────┤ lingering ├───────────────────┘
+      │                            └───────────┘
+      │                                  │ operator stop / runtime exit / fatal
+      │                                  ▼
+      └──────────────────────────── draining ──► exited
+```
+
+| State | Socket bound | Accepts `acquire` | Accepts `status` | Accepts `stop` |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `absent` | no | n/a | n/a | n/a |
+| `starting-unverified` | no | n/a | n/a | **no — reported, never signalled** |
+| `starting` | **no** | n/a | n/a | n/a |
+| `serving` | yes | yes | yes | yes |
+| `lingering` | yes | yes (cancels linger) | yes | yes |
+| `draining` | yes | no — `shared_runtime_shutting_down` | yes | idempotent |
+| `exited` | unlinked | n/a | n/a | n/a |
+
+`starting-unverified` is new in revision 4 and is an **observer-side** state, not
+a broker-side one: it is what an operator sees when `broker.lock` is held and no
+owner record can be read. Revision 3 had no name for it, asserted that no broker
+of this protocol could produce it, and refused. The assertion was false — B2
+acquires the lock and B4 publishes, and a broker stopped or hard-blocked in
+between is exactly this state (probe P16.A). Revision 4 does three things about
+it and claims nothing more: it shrinks the window to one atomic write with no
+blocking call in it (section 6.2); it reports the state by name, together with
+any kernel-visible candidate holders and their `p_stat`, so a stopped broker is
+visible rather than invisible (section 10.1); and it bounds the operator path by
+re-polling before refusing, because the two benign resolutions — the record
+appears, or the lock frees — are both self-resolving (section 10.2). What it
+does **not** do is pick a pid to signal: probe P16.D shows the candidate set can
+hold two processes that are indistinguishable on uid, binary inode, argv and
+`p_stat`, and only one of them holds the lock.
+
+The socket is bound **only after** the runtime is ready and attested. Therefore
+"a connectable rendezvous socket exists" implies "a ready attested runtime
+exists", and no client ever observes a half-started broker. Startup
+serialization is the `broker.lock` in section 6, not the socket.
+
+The corollary is what section 6 must handle and revision 1 did not. During
+`starting` a peer observes **no socket**, and any broker it offers exits
+`EXIT_ELECTION_LOST` because the incumbent holds `broker.lock`. That pair is not
+an error condition. It is the normal, expected observation for every client that
+arrives during another client's runtime startup, which for a 27B MLX model is a
+window of tens of seconds, and it must resolve to "wait and attach", never to a
+timeout. Revision 3 removes the client from the lock entirely: a client learns
+"a peer owns this runtime" from the exit status of its own broker child, which
+is a fact the kernel hands it about its own process, not a filesystem
+observation it has to interpret.
+
+**First-lease grace.** A broker that reaches `serving` and has never granted a
+lease drains after `broker_start_timeout_seconds` measured from B14. This bounds
+the one case the lease-driven lifecycle does not cover: a client that forks a
+broker and then dies, or times out, before attaching. Without it such a broker
+would hold the runtime forever, since `lingering` is only ever entered on the
+transition from one lease to zero. The grace is a fixed derivation of an existing
+field, not a new configuration knob.
+
+Revision 4 makes the grace load-bearing rather than a tidy-up. It is now the
+**only** thing that bounds a broker whose starter gave up, because a client at
+its deadline no longer signals anything (section 6.1 step 4d, review finding F2).
+Probe P19.D is the control for that: with the client's shutdown authority removed,
+an unused broker still drains, reaps its runtime and unlinks its socket.
+
+### 3.2 Lease states
+
+```
+  requested ──accept──► held ──release / peer close / broker drain──► released
+      │                  │
+      └──refuse──►       └──heartbeat overdue──► held(stale)  (reported, NOT revoked)
+```
+
+`held(stale)` is an observability state only. A lease leaves `held` exclusively
+on connection teardown, an explicit `release`, or a broker drain. Rationale: the
+event that must free the runtime is client **death**, and connection teardown
+detects death exactly, including `SIGKILL`. A timer would add false positives
+that kill a live agent mid-request. A wedged-but-alive client is bounded by
+`agents-infra runtime stop --force`, never by a silent timer.
+
+## 4. Configuration contract
+
+```toml
+[agents.pi.profiles."qwen-3.8-27b-mlx-8bit".runtime.sharing]
+mode = "shared"
+linger_seconds = 0
+max_leases = 8
+heartbeat_interval_seconds = 15
+lease_stale_seconds = 60
+broker_start_timeout_seconds = 240
+```
+
+- The whole `[runtime.sharing]` table is optional. Absent ⇒ `mode = "exclusive"`
+  ⇒ every behavior in `.research/260817_pi-local-model-launch-contract.md`
+  section 7 applies unchanged, including `runtime_listener_occupied` and
+  `pi_profile_busy`.
+- When the table is present, **every** listed field is required and unknown
+  fields fail closed, matching the existing profile schema discipline. No field
+  receives an invented default; `max_leases` in particular is an operator
+  capacity decision on a single-accelerator host, not something agents-infra
+  guesses.
+- `mode` is exactly `"exclusive"` or `"shared"`.
+- `linger_seconds >= 0`. `max_leases >= 1`.
+  `0 < heartbeat_interval_seconds < lease_stale_seconds`.
+  `broker_start_timeout_seconds >= runtime.startup_timeout_seconds +
+  runtime.shutdown_timeout_seconds + 30`.
+
+  That constraint is deliberately stronger than revision 2's, and the reason is
+  review finding F4. Because the sharing table is outside `profile_digest`, two
+  authorized clients may legally carry different `broker_start_timeout_seconds`,
+  while a waiter necessarily bounds its own wait with its own value. Revision 2
+  left open whether a legitimate peer start could be refused purely because the
+  waiter was configured less patiently. It cannot, and the schema is what
+  guarantees it: both `runtime.startup_timeout_seconds` and
+  `runtime.shutdown_timeout_seconds` **are** inside `profile_digest`, so every
+  client authorized to share this runtime resolves the same two values, and the
+  `+ 30` covers the bounded non-model work — profile composition, predecessor
+  reclamation, port preflight, the fork, the record writes and poll granularity.
+  The **smallest admissible** value is therefore already an upper bound on a
+  correct startup, identically for every authorized client. Divergence above it
+  means one client is merely more patient, never that a correct start is refused.
+  The two timeout outcomes are told apart by refusal code
+  (`broker_start_timeout` versus `shared_runtime_peer_start_timeout`), not by a
+  second knob, and `shared_runtime_peer_start_timeout` carries both the waiter's
+  own configured value and the broker's effective one so an asymmetry is visible
+  rather than inferred.
+- `max_leases`, `linger_seconds`, and the heartbeat fields are **capacity and
+  timing policy, not identity**, so they are excluded from `profile_digest` and
+  two projects with byte-identical profiles but divergent sharing policy share
+  one broker. The broker started first fixes the values that apply; section 5.4
+  states the rule and how the divergence is surfaced.
+- Composition follows the existing atomic per-profile rule: a nearer definition
+  of the same exact profile name replaces the ancestor definition whole; sharing
+  fields never merge across files.
+- There is no CLI override for any sharing field and no environment override for
+  any path, socket, timeout, or mode. An override would be a bypass around the
+  gates in section 7.
+
+Traceability: `TASK-260825-lsojra` scope "reuse canonical target/profile
+resolution and managed runtime identity checks".
+
+## 5. Identity, keys, and paths
+
+### 5.1 Digests
+
+`profile_digest` is `SHA-256` over the concatenation, in exactly this order, of
+records encoded as `name || 0x00 || uint64be(len(value)) || value || 0x1E`:
+
+```
+schema                = "agents-infra.pi.shared-runtime.profile.v1"
+provider, model, base_url, api, readiness_path
+reasoning, thinking, context_window, max_tokens        (decimal ASCII)
+input[i]                                                (i ascending, one record each)
+compat.<member>                                         (byte-sorted member names)
+runtime.executable
+runtime.argc                                            (decimal ASCII)
+runtime.argv[i]                                         (i ascending, one record each)
+runtime.startup_timeout_seconds, runtime.shutdown_timeout_seconds
+runtime.dflash.target_model, .draft_model               (absent ⇒ record omitted)
+runtime.dflash.target_argv[i], .draft_argv[i]
+```
+
+`exec_plan_digest` (section 6.2, B10) uses the **same record encoding** over a
+different, smaller field list — `runtime.executable`, `runtime.argc`, each
+`runtime.argv[i]`, `runtime.cwd`, `runtime.startup_timeout_seconds` — because it
+answers a narrower question: whether the durable record and the process it names
+describe the same plan. Every field it covers except `runtime.cwd` is already
+inside `profile_digest`, which is why `runtime_key` equality is what actually
+pins the launcher's plan to the broker's and the digest is only a consistency
+check (section 9).
+
+Length-prefixing makes the encoding injective, so no separator ambiguity, JSON
+key ordering, or Unicode normalization can make two byte-distinct profiles hash
+alike. `requested_capabilities` is derived from the above and is not an
+independent record. The `[runtime.sharing]` table is **not** part of the digest:
+capacity and timing policy do not change which runtime is correct, and including
+them would needlessly split otherwise-identical runtimes. Two projects with
+byte-identical profiles but divergent sharing policy therefore share one broker;
+section 5.4 states whose policy governs and how the divergence is reported.
+
+`runtime_key = SHA-256("agents-infra.pi.shared-runtime.v1" || 0x00 ||
+utf8(base_url) || 0x00 || profile_digest)`, lowercase hex, 64 characters.
+
+The runtime key deliberately **excludes the project**. `127.0.0.1:<port>` is a
+machine-global resource: keying brokers per project would make two projects with
+byte-identical profiles deadlock each other on the same port, which is the exact
+outcome this story exists to remove. Two projects share a runtime only when
+their composed profile bytes and endpoint are byte-identical, which means both
+project configurations authorized the same executable with the same argv. A
+divergent profile on the same port is refused (section 7), never attached.
+
+Client state keys are unchanged and remain project-scoped:
+`project_key = SHA-256(canonical_project_path)`,
+`profile_key = SHA-256(exact decoded profile name bytes)`,
+`run_key = SHA-256("agents-infra.pi.run.v1" || 0x00 || utf8(TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID))`.
+
+### 5.2 Paths
+
+| Path | Purpose | Scope |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `<cache>/agents-infra/pi/<project_key>/<profile_key>/{agent,sessions,session.lock}` | default client state | unchanged |
+| `<cache>/agents-infra/pi/<project_key>/<profile_key>/runs/<run_key>/{agent,sessions,client.lock}` | RUN-scoped client state | new |
+| `<cache>/agents-infra/pi-runtimes/<runtime_key>/broker.lock` | single-flight + broker liveness | new |
+| `<cache>/agents-infra/pi-runtimes/<runtime_key>/broker-state.json` | owner record and crash-recovery record: published at B4 as the broker's first side effect after the election, promoted at B7, extended with the runtime block at B10 | new |
+| `<cache>/agents-infra/pi-runtimes/<runtime_key>/leases/<lease_id>.json` | lease mirror for offline status | new |
+| `<cache>/agents-infra/pi-runtimes/<runtime_key>/broker.log` | broker stdout/stderr | new |
+| `<cache>/agents-infra/pi-runtimes/<runtime_key>/runtime.log` | launcher and runtime stdout/stderr | new |
+| `<cache>/agents-infra/pi-runtimes/<runtime_key>/cwd/` | runtime working directory | new |
+| `<cache>/agents-infra/pi-rv/<runtime_key[0:32]>/b.sock` | rendezvous address | new |
+
+All new components are created and reopened through the existing anchored
+discipline: open the canonical cache root with `O_DIRECTORY|O_NOFOLLOW|O_CLOEXEC`,
+walk or create each managed component with `openat`/`mkdirat` mode `0700` and
+`O_NOFOLLOW`, `fstat`-revalidate every opened component, and prove the composed
+path is the exact expected relative suffix beneath the canonical cache root with
+every hash component matching `[0-9a-f]{64}` (or `[0-9a-f]{32}` for the
+rendezvous component). Any lookup, canonicalization, stat, containment, symlink,
+type, permission, partial-read, or revalidation failure is
+`shared_runtime_state_path_invalid` — never absence, never permission to start a
+second runtime.
+
+The rendezvous directory is `0700`; the socket inode is `0600`. The composed
+socket path is measured before `bind`; if it exceeds the platform `sun_path`
+capacity the launch fails `broker_rendezvous_path_too_long`. There is no
+environment escape hatch and no silent shortening.
+
+**The 32-hex rendezvous component is an address, never an identity.** A
+truncation collision cannot admit a wrong runtime because section 7 requires the
+full `runtime_key`, `profile_digest`, and endpoint to be byte-equal over the
+handshake before any lease is granted.
+
+The runtime's working directory in shared mode is the managed empty `cwd/`
+directory, not a project directory. A runtime shared across projects must not be
+anchored to one project's tree, and an empty managed directory is deterministic
+and leaks nothing. Exclusive mode keeps `Dir = project` exactly as today. The
+effective cwd is part of the attested runtime record.
+
+### 5.3 Per-client state isolation
+
+- `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` present and non-empty ⇒ the client uses
+  `runs/<run_key>/{agent,sessions}` and holds `runs/<run_key>/client.lock`
+  exclusively. Distinct tracked spawns therefore never share Pi agent state,
+  settings, trust, or sessions.
+- `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` absent ⇒ the client uses the existing per-profile
+  `{agent,sessions}` and holds the existing `session.lock` exclusively, which
+  preserves today's stable interactive session directory and today's
+  `pi_profile_busy` behavior for a second default client.
+- A second client presenting the **same** `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` fails
+  `pi_profile_busy` naming the RUN, because the same run identity must map to
+  the same stable state and two writers of one Pi agent directory is a lost
+  update, not isolation.
+- `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` is only ever a hash input; it is never a path component,
+  so no value can traverse. Forging another run's id is a same-uid attack and is
+  out of the threat model per section 9, but the client records the raw id in
+  diagnostics so a collision is visible.
+- Default and RUN-scoped clients coexist freely: their state keys are disjoint
+  and neither takes the other's lock.
+
+Traceability: `TASK-260825-lsojra` checklist item "Keep Pi state/session/process
+groups isolated across independent RUN owners".
+
+### 5.4 Effective sharing policy
+
+Because `[runtime.sharing]` is excluded from `profile_digest` (section 5.1), a
+broker can serve a client whose configured `max_leases`, `linger_seconds`, or
+heartbeat timings differ from its own. The rule is:
+
+- **The starter's policy governs, for the whole lifetime of that broker.** The
+  broker captures it at startup, records it in `broker-state.json`, and never
+  re-reads it. A later client cannot raise `max_leases`, cannot change
+  `linger_seconds`, and does not restart the broker to get its own values.
+- The broker announces the **effective policy** in `hello_ok`, alongside the
+  broker pid and start time that identify which client established it.
+- A divergence is **not** a refusal. It changes no correctness property: the
+  runtime is the same runtime, and the digest already proved that.
+- A divergence is never silent. When the client's configured policy differs from
+  the announced effective policy, the client reports both, and:
+  - `agents-infra runtime status` prints `sharing.effective` and, when the
+    resolved profile's own values differ, `sharing.configured` beside it with
+    the broker pid and start time that fixed the effective values;
+  - the `shared_runtime_lease_limit` refusal carries `effective_max_leases`,
+    `configured_max_leases`, the broker pid, and the broker start time, so a
+    client configured `max_leases = 16` and refused by a broker started under
+    `max_leases = 1` is told exactly why its own value did not apply and that
+    `agents-infra runtime stop` is the remedy.
+
+`linger_seconds` is the load-bearing case, because it is the only sharing field
+that can keep a runtime alive after the final lease. A peer project's nonzero
+linger would hold the runtime past the last release and defeat the section 13
+cleanup proof. The acceptance proof therefore asserts the **effective** value
+(section 13, item 9), read from `runtime status`, not the value the proving
+client happened to configure.
+
+`broker_start_timeout_seconds` is the one sharing field a **waiter** must apply
+from its own configuration rather than from the broker's, because it bounds a
+wait that happens before any handshake exists. That is not a hole in the rule
+above, and section 4 says why: the schema constraint
+`broker_start_timeout_seconds >= runtime.startup_timeout_seconds +
+runtime.shutdown_timeout_seconds + 30` is expressed entirely in digest-covered
+values plus a constant, so the smallest value any authorized client may carry is
+already an upper bound on a correct startup. A more patient peer waits longer; a
+less patient one still waits long enough. When the two differ, the
+`shared_runtime_peer_start_timeout` refusal carries both this client's configured
+value and the broker's effective one, and `runtime status` prints both, so the
+asymmetry is reported rather than inferred.
+
+Traceability: closes review finding F3 (`RUN-260825-626994`) and finding F4
+(`RUN-260825-ac68af`) against `STORY-260825-1r7z9o` AC "stop/reap the runtime
+only after the final tracked lease expires or an operator explicitly stops it".
+
+## 6. Startup, single-flight, and terminal independence
+
+Revision 4 rewrites this section again, and for the same reason revision 3 did:
+the third review (`RUN-260825-969723`) found three places where the protocol
+asked a process to infer something it could not see. Two were blocking.
+
+- the **launcher** was told a `runtime_key` and then required to apply a timeout
+  and execute an executable and argv that nothing supplied. A SHA-256 cannot be
+  inverted, so `runtime-launch` was not implementable from its declared inputs
+  (finding F1);
+- the **client** read "my broker child is still alive" as "my broker child is
+  still starting" and terminated it. The broker stays the starter's child for its
+  whole life, so that inference could revoke a peer's live lease (finding F2);
+- the **broker** owned the runtime from the instant it took `broker.lock` but
+  became identifiable only several steps later, and the specification called the
+  gap impossible instead of naming it (finding F3).
+
+Each is closed by removing the inference, not by inspecting harder: the launcher
+recomposes its own target, the client acts on nothing at its deadline, and the
+broker publishes before it does anything else.
+
+### 6.0 Broker step renumbering, revision 3 → revision 4
+
+Two steps were inserted and the detachment gate was hoisted, so **every** `B<n>`
+label moved. This table is normative for reading any revision-3 artifact,
+including the sibling checklist items that name revision-3 steps.
+
+| rev 3 | rev 4 | Step |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| B2 | **B1** | Detachment self-gate (`getsid(0) == getpid()`) — hoisted above the election |
+| B1 | **B2** | Election (`flock(LOCK_EX\|LOCK_NB)`) |
+| — | **B3** | *New.* Read the predecessor record into memory |
+| — | **B4** | *New.* Publish the elected-owner record, carrying the predecessor's runtime block forward |
+| B3 | **B5** | Self-identity: full profile composition, key verification, own-binary stat |
+| B4 | **B6** | Reclaim the carried-forward runtime block; then unlink a stale rendezvous inode |
+| B5 | **B7** | Promote the owner record to `stage = "composed"` |
+| B6 | **B8** | Port preflight |
+| B7 | **B9** | Create the runtime as an unauthorized launcher |
+| B8 | **B10** | Publish the runtime block |
+| B9 | **B11** | Authorize |
+| B10 | **B12** | The launcher's own gates |
+| B11 | **B13** | Readiness |
+| B12 | **B14** | Mark serving |
+| B13 | **B15** | Bind and accept |
+
+Why the two insertions and the hoist are not cosmetic:
+
+- **B1 above B2** because B3 and B4 both touch the shared record, and a broker
+  that wins the election only to fail the detachment gate would have read and
+  overwritten the predecessor's record on its way out. The gate is a pure
+  `getsid` call with no side effect, so there was never a reason for it to run
+  second.
+- **B3 and B4 before B5-B6** because that is finding F3: ownership must not
+  precede identity. Publishing after composition and reclamation left the window
+  open for as long as reclaiming a 27B runtime takes.
+- **B4 carries the predecessor's runtime block forward** because `broker-state.json`
+  is one file. A publication that dropped the block would erase the only record of
+  a predecessor's still-live runtime before B6 reclaimed it — reintroducing
+  finding F1's forbidden state through the door F3's fix opened. Probe P17.A
+  reproduces exactly that; P17.B is the carry-forward.
+
+### 6.1 Client algorithm
+
+In order, with no side effect before every static gate passes. **The client
+never opens `broker.lock`, and the client never signals a broker.**
+
+1. Resolve project, profile, Pi argv, Pi execution identity, runtime executable
+   identity, endpoint, and client state paths exactly as today.
+2. If `mode = "exclusive"`, run the existing cycle-8 path unchanged and stop.
+3. Compute `profile_digest`, `runtime_key`, and the rendezvous path; create the
+   client state tree; take the client lock (section 5.3).
+4. **Wait loop.** `deadline = now + broker_start_timeout_seconds`,
+   `backoff = 25ms`, `broker_child = none`, `election_lost_seen = false`,
+   `election_backoff = 2s`, `next_attempt_at = now`.
+
+   a. **Attach attempt** — `connect()` the rendezvous socket. Evaluated on
+      **every** iteration, never only the first.
+      - Success ⇒ run the section 7 handshake and `acquire`; on success continue
+        at step 5.
+      - `ENOENT` ⇒ no broker has bound yet. Continue to (b).
+      - `ECONNREFUSED` ⇒ a socket inode exists with nobody accepting: a stale
+        inode from a dead broker. Continue to (b). Only a broker holding
+        `broker.lock` may unlink it (section 6.2, B6).
+      - Any other error ⇒ `broker_unreachable`. Never treated as absence.
+   b. **Reap** — if `broker_child != none`, `wait4(WNOHANG)`:
+      - still running ⇒ **a broker of ours has not exited.** Nothing more may be
+        concluded from this; see step 4d. Go to (d).
+      - exited `EXIT_ELECTION_LOST` ⇒ **a peer's broker owns this runtime.** Set
+        `election_lost_seen = true`, `broker_child = none`,
+        `next_attempt_at = now + election_backoff`, and then
+        `election_backoff = min(election_backoff * 2, 30s)`. This client started
+        nothing and undid nothing; it simply keeps attaching through (a).
+      - exited `0` ⇒ our broker drained normally between the fork and now. Set
+        `broker_child = none` and allow an immediate further attempt.
+      - any other exit status ⇒ `broker_start_failed`, carrying the exit status
+        and the tail of `broker.log`. Immediate, not after the full deadline.
+   c. **Broker creation** — if `broker_child == none` and
+      `now >= next_attempt_at`, fork a broker (section 6.3).
+
+      There is deliberately **no fixed attempt cap**. The growing
+      `election_backoff` already bounds the rate — over a 240-second startup a
+      waiting client offers on the order of ten brokers, each of which performs
+      one `getsid`, one `open` and one `flock` and exits — and a hard cap would
+      silently break waiter promotion: a client that exhausted its attempts early
+      could not start a broker when the incumbent died late in the window.
+      Instead the backoff is **reset to 2s** whenever the client reads
+      `broker-state.json` and finds that the broker pid it names is not live
+      (absent from `kern.proc.pid`, or `p_stat == SZOMB`). That read is the
+      client's only use of the record, it takes no lock, it changes nothing, and
+      it never authorizes a start on its own — it only decides how soon this
+      client offers its next broker, which the B2 election then accepts or
+      refuses. A read that fails is not a reset.
+   d. **Deadline** — if `now >= deadline`, **refuse, and signal nothing.**
+
+      This is the whole of review finding F2, and it is a deletion rather than a
+      repair. Revision 3 read `broker_child != none` as "my broker is still
+      starting" and sent it `SIGTERM`. That reading is false: section 6.3 keeps
+      the broker this client's OS **child for its entire life**, not only while
+      it starts. The interleaving the reviewer demonstrated needs no attacker and
+      no unusual timing — A's last `connect()` returns `ENOENT`; the broker
+      completes B15 and binds; an independent client B attaches and takes a
+      lease; A reaches its deadline, sees a live child and terminates it; the
+      broker drains and B's lease is revoked with no operator stop. Probe P19.A
+      reproduces it end to end, and probe P19.B shows that A's `wait4(WNOHANG)`
+      returns the **identical** answer in the run where the broker is starting
+      and the run where it is serving. A signal that cannot tell those apart is
+      not a remedy; it is a bypass around section 7.5's final-release rule.
+
+      So the branch selects a refusal code from facts this client owns, and takes
+      no action on any process:
+      - `broker_child != none` ⇒ **`broker_start_timeout`**. The refusal payload
+        carries the child's pid and the raw `wait4(WNOHANG)` result, and reports
+        the broker's state as **`unknown`** — not `starting`, which is the
+        inference that caused the defect. The remedy printed is
+        `agents-infra runtime status` followed, if needed, by
+        `agents-infra runtime stop --force`, which are the surfaces that *can*
+        establish the state, because they read the owner record and attest what
+        they find.
+      - otherwise `election_lost_seen` ⇒ **`shared_runtime_peer_start_timeout`**:
+        every broker this client offered lost the election to a peer's broker,
+        which never became connectable. This client started nothing and must not.
+        Same remedy.
+      - otherwise ⇒ **`broker_start_timeout`** with no child.
+
+      Nothing is stranded by the deletion, and the bound is not a promise made
+      on trust:
+      - a broker wedged **before** `serving` is bounded by its own steps. Every
+        one of B5-B13 is bounded — composition by I/O, reclamation by
+        `runtime.shutdown_timeout_seconds`, preflight by a single `bind`,
+        readiness by `runtime.startup_timeout_seconds` — and each fatal outcome
+        removes the broker's own record and releases the lock by exiting;
+      - a broker that reached `serving` and granted no lease is bounded by the
+        **first-lease grace** (section 3.1), verified by probe P19.D;
+      - a broker that reached `serving` and granted a lease is **not** this
+        client's to end, which is the entire point;
+      - a broker that is stopped or hard-blocked is `starting-unverified` and is
+        an operator matter (section 10.2). A `SIGTERM` from a client would not
+        have reached a `SIGSTOP`ped process anyway.
+
+      The client does not `wait()` the abandoned child. It exits, at which point
+      the child reparents to the init process; a long-lived client that keeps
+      running reaps opportunistically with `WNOHANG` and never blocks.
+   e. Sleep `backoff`, then `backoff = min(backoff * 3 / 2, 250ms)`.
+
+5. The lease is held. Run Pi. On Pi exit or on `SIGINT`/`SIGTERM`, send
+   `release`, close the connection, and exit.
+
+`broker_start_timeout_seconds` bounds the whole loop for both outcomes, measured
+from the first attach attempt; section 4 states why its smallest admissible value
+is already an upper bound on a correct startup for every authorized client.
+
+Two properties of this loop are worth naming, because revision 2 needed
+dedicated machinery for both:
+
+- **Waiter promotion is free.** If the incumbent broker dies, the kernel releases
+  `broker.lock`, and the next client whose backoff expires forks a broker that
+  simply wins. There is no promotion path to get wrong (probe P8.C).
+- **A losing broker is cheap and harmless.** It performs one `getsid`, one `open`
+  and one `flock` and exits, before reclamation, preflight, runtime creation and
+  bind (probes P8.A/P8.B, P20.A). Over a 240-second 27B startup a waiting client
+  offers on the order of ten such processes.
+
+### 6.2 Broker algorithm
+
+The broker's steps are normative and their **order** is normative. Each step
+names what a `SIGKILL` at that instant leaves behind; section 6.4 enumerates the
+whole set. See section 6.0 for the revision-3 → revision-4 label mapping.
+
+- **B1 — Detachment self-gate.** Require `getsid(0) == getpid()`, else exit
+  `broker_not_session_leader`, **before opening or reading anything**. This is
+  what makes `agents-infra runtime broker` not an ad-hoc command, and it is a
+  direct kernel fact about the calling process. A hand-typed invocation from a
+  shell inherits the shell's session and is refused; a `Setsid` child is its own
+  session leader and is admitted (probe P9, with both negative shapes).
+
+  Revision 4 hoists this above the election. Revision 3 ran it second, which was
+  harmless only because the election had no side effect either; once B3 reads and
+  B4 writes the shared record, a broker that wins the election and then fails this
+  gate would have overwritten a predecessor's record on its way out.
+
+- **B2 — Election.** Open `broker.lock` (anchored, `O_NOFOLLOW`, regular file,
+  link count 1, `0600`) and attempt `flock(LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB)`.
+  - `EWOULDBLOCK` ⇒ exit `EXIT_ELECTION_LOST` immediately, with **no side effect
+    of any kind**: nothing read, nothing written, nothing killed, no port
+    touched, no socket bound.
+  - Any other error ⇒ exit `shared_runtime_state_path_invalid`.
+  - Acquired ⇒ this process is the runtime's owner. The descriptor stays open for
+    the broker's whole life, so the kernel releases the lock if and only if the
+    broker dies, including under `SIGKILL`, with no timer anywhere.
+
+  This single step replaces revision 2's inherited-descriptor gate in full. There
+  is nothing to infer and no descriptor 3 to attack: the broker holds the lock
+  because it acquired it, in its own process, on its own descriptor. The
+  reviewer's F2 shape — a descriptor open on the correct `broker.lock` inode but
+  not holding it, while a peer holds it and the runtime port is still free — is
+  refused here on the lock alone (probe P8.D), and the same entry point with no
+  incumbent acquires (probe P8.E), so the gate discriminates rather than always
+  refusing. Probe P20.A adds the assertion that a loser leaves the incumbent's
+  bound socket **inode** byte-identical.
+
+- **B3 — Read the predecessor record into memory.** Read `broker-state.json`
+  **before writing anything at all**, and keep the parsed value for B6. B4
+  overwrites the file, so this is the last instant at which the predecessor's
+  statement is readable.
+  - Absent ⇒ nothing to reclaim. Under revisions 3 and 4 this is a *derived*
+    fact, not an assumption: B4 precedes B9, so any predecessor that got far
+    enough to create a process also published an owner record first (probe
+    P12.B).
+  - Unreadable, partial, or malformed ⇒ `shared_runtime_state_unreadable`,
+    fatal, **before B4 writes**. A failed read is never an absence, never
+    authorizes a runtime start, and — new in revision 4 — can never destroy the
+    predecessor's record either, precisely because it is fatal at this step
+    rather than at B6 (probe P17.D).
+
+- **B4 — Publish the elected-owner record.** Atomically write
+  `broker-state.json` (`0600`, temp + `renameat` + `fsync`) with:
+
+  ```
+  stage        = "elected"
+  state        = "starting"
+  protocol_version
+  broker.pid, broker.pgid, broker.sid, broker.start_time, broker.uid
+  broker.exec_path, broker.argv                 exactly as read from the kernel
+  broker.executable_identity = { dev, ino, size, mode }   stat of its own binary
+  runtime_key_claimed                            the --runtime-key argument,
+                                                 MARKED UNVERIFIED until B5
+  runtime = <the runtime block read at B3, verbatim, with
+             stage = "inherited-unreclaimed">   or omitted if B3 found none
+  ```
+
+  Two things about this step are load-bearing and neither was in revision 3.
+
+  **It is the first side effect of the whole broker, and it is one atomic write.**
+  From this instant a live broker is identifiable — by an operator with no socket
+  to connect to (section 10.2), and by a successor with nothing to infer. Review
+  finding F3 was that revision 3 published only at what is now B7, after full
+  composition, predecessor reclamation and the port preflight, so a valid broker
+  paused anywhere in that interval held the lock with no record while
+  `stop --force` declared the state impossible. Revision 4 does not claim to have
+  closed the window — a `SIGSTOP` between B2 and B4 still produces it (probe
+  P16.A) — it narrows the window to a single `renameat` containing no blocking
+  call, names the residual state `starting-unverified`, and specifies a bounded,
+  non-signalling operator path for it.
+
+  **The predecessor's runtime block is carried forward verbatim.** Dropping it
+  would erase the only durable name of a runtime that may still be alive, and a
+  `SIGKILL` between B4 and B6 would then leave exactly the state section 6.4
+  forbids. This is not hypothetical: probe P17.A reproduces it — predecessor
+  runtime alive, successor's record present, reclamation verdict
+  `no-runtime-block` — and P17.B shows the carry-forward reclaiming at the same
+  kill instant. The block stays `inherited-unreclaimed` until B6 resolves it, and
+  a successor treats an inherited block exactly as it treats its own.
+
+  `runtime_key_claimed` is explicitly unverified here because B5 has not run. It
+  is safe to publish because it authorizes nothing and because the record is
+  already inside a path derived from that key (section 5.2), so it cannot be
+  found under any other runtime's directory.
+
+- **B5 — Self-identity.** Re-run the **full profile composition** for
+  `--profile NAME` from the `--profile-project DIR` directory — the same
+  resolution chain the client used, not a single file — and recompute
+  `profile_digest` and `runtime_key`. A mismatch against `--runtime-key` is fatal
+  before any process is created, so a forged argument cannot repoint a broker;
+  the broker exits under the discipline below, which — because the record it
+  published at B4 may still carry an unreclaimed predecessor block — means it
+  leaves the record alone rather than removing it. The source is read exactly
+  once, here; the digest is then fixed for this broker's lifetime and later edits
+  to that project's configuration cannot retroactively change what it is serving.
+  The broker's own-binary `dev/ino/size/mode` was already resolved and stat'd once
+  at B4 and published there; gate 3b (section 7.2) announces **that** recorded
+  value and this step does not re-stat it. Re-stat'ing here would defeat the gate
+  exactly the way probe P5 shows a `rename`-over upgrade defeats gate 3.
+
+- **B6 — Reclaim.** Run the reclamation subroutine against the runtime block
+  carried into the record at B4 — the value read at B3, not a re-read.
+  - No runtime block ⇒ nothing to reclaim.
+  - Present ⇒ run the subroutine.
+
+  **Reclamation subroutine.** Against the recorded `pid`, `pgid`, `start_time`,
+  `uid`, `pre_exec` and `post_exec` identity shapes:
+  1. `kern.proc.pid` fails ⇒ the process is gone and reaped ⇒ **stale record**;
+     drop the block.
+  2. `p_stat == SZOMB` ⇒ an exited, unreaped corpse, not a runtime ⇒ **stale
+     record**; drop the block. Probe P15: `kern.proc.pid` answers for a zombie,
+     so omitting this check would let one corpse block every future start
+     forever.
+  3. `uid` differs from the caller's effective uid ⇒
+     `shared_runtime_orphan_unidentifiable`, fatal. Do not signal, do not start.
+     The record is left intact, **including** the unreclaimed block, so the next
+     broker inherits the same unresolved orphan and reaches the same refusal.
+     A deterministic repeated refusal is the correct outcome; silently forgetting
+     the orphan is not.
+  4. `start_time` differs from the record ⇒ pid reuse ⇒ **stale record**; drop
+     the block. A bare pid is never acted upon (probe P13.A).
+  5. Exec path and exact argv match **neither** the recorded `pre_exec` shape nor
+     the recorded `post_exec` shape ⇒ `shared_runtime_orphan_unidentifiable`,
+     fatal, with the same record-preserving rule as step 3. The process occupying
+     that pid with that start time is ours by construction, so an unrecognized
+     identity is a contradiction to report, not a thing to guess about (probe
+     P13.B).
+  6. Otherwise it is a genuine **orphan** left by a `SIGKILL`ed broker: signal
+     its **process group** `SIGTERM`, escalate to `SIGKILL` after
+     `runtime.shutdown_timeout_seconds`, require the pid to become not-live under
+     the corrected predicate, and require the endpoint to become free. Failure ⇒
+     `runtime_shutdown_timeout`, fatal. The orphan is **never attached to**: no
+     live process is its parent, so the direct-child invariant cannot be
+     restored (probes P12.C, P12.D).
+
+  On a stale or reclaimed verdict the broker rewrites the record without the
+  runtime block. Only after reclamation resolves may the broker unlink a stale
+  rendezvous socket inode. Holding `broker.lock` is what makes that safe: a live
+  broker holds the lock, so a process that reaches this point cannot be unlinking
+  a live socket.
+
+- **B7 — Promote the owner record.** Atomically rewrite `broker-state.json` with
+  `stage = "composed"`, the **verified** `runtime_key` and `profile_digest`, the
+  endpoint, and the effective sharing policy (section 5.4). `state` stays
+  `"starting"`. From here the record is a complete statement about a broker whose
+  configuration has been verified against its own command line.
+
+- **B8 — Port preflight.** Bind `127.0.0.1:<port>` exclusively and close the
+  probe. Occupied ⇒ `runtime_listener_occupied`, fatal, under the exit discipline
+  below.
+
+- **B9 — Create the runtime as an unauthorized launcher.** Create a `pipe()`.
+  Spawn the broker's **own executable** as
+
+  ```
+  agents-infra runtime runtime-launch --runtime-key <hex> \
+      --profile-project <dir> --profile <name>
+  ```
+
+  as the leader of a **new process group**, with cwd `cwd/`, a scrubbed
+  environment carrying no inbound `PI_*` and no denied loader variable, `stdin`
+  from `/dev/null`, `stdout`/`stderr` to the runtime log, and the pipe's **read**
+  end as `ExtraFiles[0]`, i.e. descriptor 3 in the child. The broker retains the
+  **write** end and passes no copy of it to any child; if a copy leaked, the
+  guarantee in B12 would silently evaporate (probe P11.B asserts it does not).
+
+  `--profile-project` and `--profile` are new in revision 4 and are the answer to
+  review finding F1. They are not conveniences: they are the **only** transport
+  by which the launcher learns what to execute, with what argv, in what time
+  bound. Section 10.3 states why the alternative — putting the plan in the
+  authorization frame — was rejected.
+
+  At this instant no runtime exists. The child is the broker's own binary,
+  blocked on a read, incapable of becoming anything else.
+
+- **B10 — Publish the runtime block.** Read the launcher's `pid`, `pgid`, kernel
+  `start_time` and `uid` from `kern.proc.pid`, and its current exec path and
+  exact argv from `kern.procargs2`. Atomically rewrite `broker-state.json`,
+  keeping `state = "starting"` and adding the runtime block:
+
+  ```
+  runtime.pid, runtime.pgid, runtime.start_time, runtime.uid
+  runtime.pre_exec  = { exec_path, argv }        as just read from the kernel
+  runtime.post_exec = { exec_path: runtime.executable,
+                        argv: [runtime.executable] + runtime.argv }
+  runtime.cwd, runtime.endpoint
+  runtime.executable_identity = { dev, ino, size, mode }
+  runtime.exec_plan_digest
+  runtime.stage = "authorizing"
+  ```
+
+  `exec_plan_digest` is `SHA-256` over the same length-prefixed encoding as
+  section 5.1, of exactly the values the launcher will act on:
+  `runtime.executable`, `runtime.argc`, each `runtime.argv[i]`, `runtime.cwd`,
+  and `runtime.startup_timeout_seconds`. The broker computes it from its own B5
+  composition and the launcher recomputes it from its own; see B12.
+
+  Two identity shapes are recorded because the record is written while the
+  process is still the launcher and is read, later, against the runtime. Probe
+  P10 establishes that this is sound: `execve` preserves pid, pgid and process
+  start time byte-for-byte and replaces only exec path and argv.
+
+- **B11 — Authorize.** Write exactly one authorization frame to the pipe. The
+  frame has **exactly five fields and no others**:
+
+  ```
+  schema           = "agents-infra.pi.shared-runtime.auth.v1"   compiled constant
+  protocol_version = the broker's own compiled version, `6` as of this revision
+                     (the same value announced to clients and compared at gate 5,
+                     section 7.2)
+  runtime_key      = the verified key from B5
+  launcher_pid     = the pid read from the kernel at B10
+  exec_plan_digest = the digest computed from the broker's own B5 composition
+  ```
+
+  **Only after this write can a runtime process exist**, and the record naming it
+  is already durable.
+
+  The frame answers **whether**, and for **whom**. It never answers **what**: it
+  carries no executable, no argv, no cwd and no timeout. `exec_plan_digest` is
+  not an instruction either — the launcher computes its own and compares, so the
+  field can only cause a refusal.
+
+  Neither `schema` nor `protocol_version` is decorative. Both are compiled
+  constants on the writing side and equality gates on the reading side (B12 step
+  5), and revision 5 exists because revision 4 left that claim unevidenced.
+
+  They are two fields because they answer two questions, and the last two
+  revisions are the illustration: each bumped `protocol_version` — `4` → `5` → `6`
+  — because the launcher's gate set changed, and each left `schema` at
+  `...auth.v1` because the frame's five fields and their encoding did not.
+  `schema` says *how to read these bytes*; `protocol_version` says *what the
+  broker that wrote them does*. A build that changed the frame's shape without
+  changing broker behaviour would move the first and not the second. Applying one
+  rule twice, to two changes that differ, is the only test a rule of this kind
+  gets.
+
+  The frame's field set is **closed**, and revision 6 states what that costs the
+  reader rather than only what it forbids the writer. Forbidding is the easy
+  half: an implementation may not add a field the launcher reads and does not
+  compare, may not carry a field the launcher ignores, and extends the frame only
+  through a protocol-version bump (section 9). The half revision 5 left
+  unspecified is that **closure is a property of the decode, not of the
+  comparisons**. A launcher that decodes into a five-member structure has already
+  thrown away everything it needs in order to know whether the set was closed;
+  its five comparisons are then true statements about five values and say nothing
+  about the frame. B12 step 4 is where the set is actually closed, and it is
+  specified in terms of the decoded key multiset for exactly that reason.
+
