+  importantly — a broker that is no longer there.
+
+- **B13 — Readiness.** Wait for direct-child liveness plus exact `/v1/models`
+  discovery of the configured model (`runtime.dflash.target_model` when present),
+  with `503` retried until timeout, exactly as `waitPiRuntimeReady` does today.
+  A child exit during startup ⇒ `runtime_exited_early`; timeout ⇒ terminate the
+  process group and exit `runtime_readiness_timeout`. In both cases the broker
+  reaps the group and removes its own record before exiting.
+
+- **B14 — Mark serving.** Atomically rewrite the record with `state = "serving"`,
+  `runtime.stage = "running"` and the readiness stamp. The first-lease grace
+  (section 3.1) is measured from this instant.
+
+- **B15 — Bind and accept.** Bind the rendezvous socket and begin accepting.
+  `bind` must fail closed if anything occupies the path: a live listener and a
+  stale inode both produce `EADDRINUSE` (section 2, probe P3), and the broker
+  exits `broker_rendezvous_bind_conflict` rather than unlinking. Only a broker
+  holding `broker.lock` unlinks a rendezvous inode, and only at B6. Probe P20.B
+  asserts this on the contender's `dev`/`ino` rather than on the path's
+  existence, because an unlink-and-rebind would also leave *a* socket there.
+
+The socket is bound only at B15, so **a connectable rendezvous socket implies a
+ready, attested runtime**, and no client ever observes a half-started broker.
+
+**Fatal-exit discipline.** Every fatal outcome above exits the broker, and what
+it does to `broker-state.json` on the way out is normative, because getting it
+wrong reproduces the state section 6.4 forbids by a different route. Revision 3
+said only "removes its own record", which was safe only while the record could
+never name a runtime the broker had not created. Since B4 it can.
+
+| Exiting from | Record action | Why |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| B1 – B3 | **nothing** — none was written | The predecessor's record is untouched and stays authoritative |
+| B4 – B6, record carries an unreclaimed block (inherited or own) | **leave the record exactly as it is** | The block is the only durable name of a process that may still be alive. Removing it here is precisely probe P17.A's defect reached through the fatal path instead of through a `SIGKILL`. A successor reads a record whose broker pid is dead and whose block is unreclaimed, and reclaims it — the same path it would take for any crashed predecessor |
+| B6 – B9, block resolved, no runtime created | remove the record, then exit | It names no process; leaving it would only make the next broker parse a stale owner |
+| after B9, a runtime or launcher exists | **reap first, remove second** | Signal the group `SIGTERM`, escalate to `SIGKILL` after `runtime.shutdown_timeout_seconds`, require the pid not-live under the corrected predicate and the endpoint free — **and only then** remove the record. Removing first opens a live-runtime-no-record window for the whole duration of the reap, which is the same defect measured in milliseconds instead of forever |
+| after B9, the reap **failed** (`runtime_shutdown_timeout`) | **leave the record, with the runtime block** | A broker never removes a record naming a process it could not kill. The successor inherits it and reaches its own verdict on its own evidence |
+
+The rule underneath all five rows is one sentence: **the record may only stop
+naming a process once that process is proven gone.** Section 7.5's normal drain
+already obeys it — it reaps, `wait()`s, and only then removes — and this table
+extends the same ordering to every abnormal exit.
+
+### 6.3 Broker fork subroutine
+
+Invoked from client step 4c. Re-resolve the client's own executable
+(`os.Executable` + `EvalSymlinks` + `stat`), then spawn it as
+
+```
+agents-infra runtime broker --runtime-key <hex> --profile-project <dir> --profile <name>
+```
+
+with `Setsid: true`, no controlling terminal, `stdin` from `/dev/null`,
+`stdout`/`stderr` appended to `broker.log`, and a scrubbed environment carrying
+no inbound `PI_*` and no denied loader variable. **No descriptor is inherited**;
+in particular no lock descriptor, because there is no longer any lock to hand
+over. The client does not block in `wait()`; it reaps with `WNOHANG` in step 4b.
+
+Terminal independence comes from `setsid`, not from reparenting. The broker is a
+session leader with no controlling terminal, its descriptors point at managed
+files, and no client blocks on it. A `setsid` child nonetheless remains the
+**child** of the client that forked it until that client exits, at which point it
+reparents to the init process (section 2, probe P6: `ppid` still equals the
+starter's pid after `Setsid`, and the child's session id is its own).
+
+Keeping it a real child buys exactly one thing, and revision 4 states the price
+beside it. What it buys: step 4b can distinguish `EXIT_ELECTION_LOST` from a
+genuine startup failure **at once**, instead of every client waiting out the full
+deadline. What it costs: the parent relationship outlives startup, so "my child
+is alive" is true of a broker that is serving other clients — which is precisely
+what review finding F2 exploited when revision 3 treated that fact as licence to
+signal. Revision 4 keeps the child relationship and removes the signal (step 4d).
+The relationship is now read for **exit status only**, never for liveness
+semantics. A double-fork would buy immediate reparenting and lose the exit
+status; it is rejected in section 14. At most one unreaped broker child can exist
+per client, and the kernel reaps it when the client exits.
+
+### 6.4 The crash-window enumeration
+
+This is the direct answer to the second review's finding F1, stated as a closed
+set rather than as a claim, and re-derived in revision 4 for the new step
+numbering and for the carry-forward rule. For a broker `SIGKILL`ed at each
+instant:
+
+| Killed at | Runtime process left behind | Durable record | Successor's verdict |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| before B1 | none | the predecessor's, or absent | unchanged |
+| B1 – B2 | none | the predecessor's, **untouched** — B1 reads and writes nothing | unchanged |
+| B2 – B4 | none created by this broker | the predecessor's, untouched — B3 only reads | reclaims the predecessor exactly as this broker would have. Observable meanwhile as `starting-unverified` (probe P16.A) |
+| B4 – B6 | none created by this broker; the **predecessor's** may still be alive | **own** owner record, `stage = elected`, carrying the predecessor's block as `inherited-unreclaimed` | reclaims the inherited block exactly as this broker would have. Without the carry-forward this row would read "record present, no runtime block" while a live runtime existed — probe P17.A reproduces that, P17.B the fix |
+| B6 – B9 | none | own record, `stage = elected` or `composed`, no runtime block | `no runtime block` ⇒ nothing to reclaim. This is an affirmative reading of a present record, not an inference from an absence (probe P12.B) |
+| B9 – B11 | an unauthorized launcher, which exits on EOF | owner record, with the runtime block if B10 completed | the launcher removes itself (P11.A). A successor finds the pid gone ⇒ stale record; or, if it arrives first, matches the `pre_exec` shape and kills the group (probe P12.C) |
+| after B11 | the runtime | owner record with the runtime block | matches the `post_exec` shape ⇒ the group is killed and exactly one replacement is started (probe P12.D) |
+| after B15 | the runtime | owner record, `state = serving` | as above; clients additionally see EOF and exit `broker_terminated` |
+
+**There is no row in which a live runtime exists and no record names it.**
+Revision 2 had exactly one such row, and probe P12.A reproduces it end to end:
+runtime alive, `broker-state.json` absent, `broker.lock` free, and reclamation's
+verdict on the absent record is `no-record` while the runtime is still serving.
+Revision 4's B4 would have opened a second such row — a predecessor's live
+runtime erased by the successor's own publication — and probe P17.A reproduces
+*that* before the carry-forward closes it. The enumeration is claimed closed for
+this step list; a reviewer looking for a seventh instant should look between B10's
+two-step rewrite and B11's write, and between B4's `renameat` and B6's first
+`kern.proc.pid`, which are the two places where the record and the world are
+momentarily described by different processes.
+
+`broker.lock` held ⟺ a broker exists or is starting. That equivalence is what
+makes single-flight exact: a second broker cannot exist, and a crashed broker
+frees the lock through the kernel with no timer and no timeout. It does **not**
+imply the holder is identifiable at that instant; that is the `starting-unverified`
+state, and section 10.2 is where the difference is handled honestly.
+
+### 6.5 Transient-broker retry rule
+
+Applies to every handshake attempt made in client step 4a. On
+`shared_runtime_shutting_down`, **or on a clean EOF or `ECONNRESET` received
+after a successful `connect()` but before `hello_ok` and `lease` have both
+completed**, the client treats the broker as draining: it waits, bounded, for the
+socket to disappear and re-enters the step 4 loop at most 3 times, then
+`shared_runtime_unavailable`.
+
+A mid-handshake EOF is a broker that drained between `connect()` and the
+handshake. It is transient by construction and must not be reported as
+`broker_unreachable` or `broker_identity_unavailable`, which are hard refusals. A
+**malformed** frame remains `protocol_violation` and is never retried, and once a
+lease has been granted an EOF is `broker_terminated`, not a retry.
+
+Re-entering the step 4 loop does not reset the `deadline`. A client that spends
+its whole budget bouncing off draining brokers refuses on the deadline like any
+other, and the `election_backoff` in step 4c still bounds how often it may offer
+a broker.
+
+## 7. Attestation and lease protocol
+
+### 7.1 Transport
+
+`SOCK_STREAM` over `AF_UNIX`, newline-delimited JSON, one JSON object per line,
+maximum 65536 bytes per line, UTF-8, no embedded newlines. Longer or malformed
+frames close the connection with `protocol_violation`. The broker enforces a
+16-connection accept backlog and a bounded per-connection read deadline.
+
+### 7.2 Handshake gates, in order
+
+Every gate is fail-closed. A gate that cannot be evaluated produces an
+`*_unavailable` refusal; it never falls through to success.
+
+| # | Gate | Client-side check | Refusal |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| 1 | Peer uid | `LOCAL_PEERCRED` uid == `geteuid()` | `broker_peer_untrusted` |
+| 2 | Peer pid liveness | `LOCAL_PEERPID` resolves through `kern.proc.pid` **and** `p_stat != SZOMB` | `broker_identity_unavailable` |
+| 3 | Broker executable | peer exec path from `kern.procargs2`, resolved and stat'd; `dev`/`ino` byte-equal to the client's own resolved executable | `broker_executable_identity_mismatch` |
+| 3b | Broker build | the `dev/ino/size/mode` the broker **recorded for its own binary at its own startup**, byte-equal to a fresh client-side `stat` of the client's own resolved executable | `broker_build_identity_mismatch` |
+| 4 | Broker start time | announced start time byte-equal to the kernel value for the peer pid | `broker_identity_mismatch` |
+| 5 | Protocol version | announced version == client's compiled version | `broker_protocol_version_mismatch` |
+| 6 | Runtime key | announced `runtime_key` byte-equal to client's computed value | `shared_runtime_identity_mismatch` |
+| 7 | Profile digest | announced `profile_digest` byte-equal to client's computed value | `shared_runtime_profile_mismatch` |
+| 8 | Endpoint | announced endpoint byte-equal to configured `base_url` | `shared_runtime_endpoint_mismatch` |
+| 9 | Runtime executable | announced `dev/ino/size/mode` byte-equal to a fresh client-side `stat` of `runtime.executable` | `runtime_executable_invalid` |
+| 10 | Runtime process | client independently reads `kern.proc.pid` and `kern.procargs2` for the announced runtime pid: uid == own uid, start time byte-equal, exec path byte-equal to `runtime.executable`, argv byte-equal to `[executable] + runtime.argv` token for token | `runtime_identity_mismatch` |
+| 11 | Runtime liveness | the same read proves the process exists now **and is not a zombie** (`p_stat != SZOMB`) | `runtime_exited_early` |
+| 12 | Model discovery | client itself performs `GET <base_url><readiness_path>` and requires the OpenAI list shape and an item whose `id` is byte-equal to the expected model | `runtime_model_unavailable` / `runtime_readiness_invalid` |
+
+Gates 2 and 11 spell out `p_stat != SZOMB` because `kern.proc.pid` alone is not
+a liveness test: it answers successfully for an exited, unreaped process (probe
+P15). A gate that accepted a corpse as a live peer or a live runtime would admit
+an endpoint backed by nothing.
+
+Gates 9-12 are performed by the client from kernel and network sources, not
+copied from the broker's word. The broker's announcement is a claim to be
+falsified, never evidence. The runtime is never asked to describe itself: every
+process fact comes from `sysctl`, and the only runtime-sourced datum is the
+`/v1/models` body, which is used solely to require an exact expected id — a
+malicious body can make discovery fail but can never make an unexpected identity
+pass.
+
+Gate 3 is what makes the whole chain bootstrap: only a process running the same
+inode as the caller's own agents-infra binary, under the same uid, can be a
+broker. A program that merely binds the rendezvous path and speaks the protocol
+correctly is refused before any lease exists.
+
+Gate 3 has a precise limit that must not be overstated, because revision 1's
+section 8 asserted a refusal that does not occur. Gate 3 compares the inode
+currently **at the peer's recorded exec path**; it does not and cannot compare
+the inode the peer is actually executing, which the kernel does not expose
+without `proc_pidinfo`. Under the ordinary in-place upgrade shape — stage a new
+file, `rename` it over the same path — a broker started from the old build now
+resolves to the *new* inode, which is exactly what the new client resolves for
+itself, so gate 3 **passes** and admits a broker running the previous build.
+Verified: section 2 probe P5.
+
+Gate 3b closes that. The broker resolves and stats its own binary once, at its
+own startup, records `dev/ino/size/mode` in `broker-state.json`, and announces
+that recorded value. A stale broker announces the pre-upgrade inode; the new
+client stats the post-upgrade inode; they differ and the client refuses
+`broker_build_identity_mismatch`, whose remedy is `agents-infra runtime stop`.
+Two clients of the *same* build always agree, so the legitimate path is
+unaffected.
+
+Gate 3b is an announced value, so it is worth stating why that is not transitive
+trust. Both gates must pass. Gate 3 is kernel-sourced and defeats a squatter: to
+pass it, a process must genuinely be executing the caller's own binary. Gate 3b
+is self-reported and defeats a stale build. An announced value can therefore only
+cause a refusal — it can never admit anything gate 3 would have rejected — which
+is the only shape in which self-reported evidence is admissible at all. The pair
+is what makes the row honest; neither alone is.
+
+The broker applies the mirror-image gates to the client: peer uid equality,
+peer executable `dev`/`ino` equality with its own, protocol version equality,
+and byte-equal `runtime_key`/`profile_digest`. A client that presents a
+divergent profile is refused `shared_runtime_profile_mismatch` rather than being
+served the wrong model.
+
+### 7.3 Messages
+
+| Direction | Message | Payload | Response |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| C→B | `hello` | protocol version, client pid, own exec `dev/ino`, `runtime_key`, `profile_digest`, `project_key`, `profile_key`, `run_id` or null, own configured sharing policy | `hello_ok` with the full attestation record, the broker's start-time-recorded binary identity (gate 3b), and the effective sharing policy (section 5.4), or `refused` |
+| C→B | `acquire` | client state key, requested at | `lease` with `lease_id`, endpoint, runtime record, `lease_count`; or `refused` |
+| C→B | `heartbeat` | `lease_id` | `heartbeat_ok` with lease state and `lease_count` |
+| C→B | `release` | `lease_id` | `released` |
+| C→B | `status` | — | `status` snapshot; no lease required |
+| C→B | `stop` | `force`, `timeout_seconds` | `stopping`, or `refused` `shared_runtime_leases_active` |
+| B→C | `runtime_exited` | exit status | connection closed after send |
+| B→C | `shutting_down` | reason | connection closed after send |
+| B→C | `lease_revoked` | `lease_id`, reason | connection closed after send |
+
+`acquire` is refused with `shared_runtime_lease_limit` when granting would
+exceed the **effective** `max_leases`, and with `shared_runtime_shutting_down`
+in `draining`. The `shared_runtime_lease_limit` payload carries
+`effective_max_leases`, the caller's `configured_max_leases`, the broker pid, and
+the broker start time, so a client refused under a peer's stricter policy is
+never left guessing why its own configured value did not apply (section 5.4).
+
+### 7.4 Lease lifetime
+
+- Grant writes `leases/<lease_id>.json` (`0600`, atomic) with `lease_id`,
+  `run_id`, `client_key`, client pid, client start time, `runtime_key`,
+  `profile_digest`, endpoint, runtime pid, grant time, and last heartbeat.
+- Release, connection teardown, revocation, or broker exit unlinks the mirror.
+- The mirror is **observability, not authority**. `agents-infra runtime status`
+  reports mirror-derived leases as `unverified` whenever the broker is
+  unreachable, because a `SIGKILL`ed broker leaves mirrors behind. A mirror is
+  never an input to a shutdown or attach decision.
+- Heartbeats every `heartbeat_interval_seconds`. Exceeding
+  `lease_stale_seconds` marks the lease `held(stale)` in status output and
+  logs it. It does not revoke.
+- The client sends `release` and closes on Pi exit and on `SIGINT`/`SIGTERM`
+  after forwarding the signal to its Pi group. In shared mode the client never
+  signals the runtime or the runtime's process group: it does not own it.
+- Since revision 4 the same rule covers the **broker**, including the one this
+  client forked. A client's only shutdown-adjacent action is `release`; every
+  other end to a broker's life comes from its own leases, its first-lease grace,
+  or an operator (section 6.1 step 4d). The broker being this client's OS child
+  is a fact about process ancestry, not a licence — review finding F2 was exactly
+  the confusion of the two, and probe P19.A is what it costs.
+
+### 7.5 Final release
+
+On the transition to zero leases the broker enters `lingering` for the
+**effective** `linger_seconds` — the starter's value, per section 5.4, not the
+value configured by whichever client happened to release last — then `draining`: it refuses new `acquire`, sends
+`shutting_down` to any connection, signals the runtime process group `SIGTERM`,
+escalates to `SIGKILL` after `runtime.shutdown_timeout_seconds`, `wait()`s the
+direct child, removes `leases/` entries and `broker-state.json`, unlinks the
+rendezvous socket, and exits — which releases `broker.lock` and returns the
+endpoint. With `linger_seconds = 0` the linger state is entered and left in the
+same scheduling step, so the acceptance proof observes an immediate, bounded
+teardown. Any nonzero linger is opt-in and is reported by `status` as
+`lingering` with the remaining seconds, so a live-with-zero-leases runtime is
+never invisible.
+
+## 8. Failure and recovery matrix
+
+| Event | Immediate effect | Recovery | Client outcome |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Client exits normally | `release` then close | none | exit code from Pi |
+| Client `SIGKILL`ed | kernel closes the connection | lease released exactly | that client's orphaned Pi loses its backend when the runtime stops; documented boundary, unchanged in kind from cycle 8 |
+| Client wedged, alive | heartbeat overdue | lease marked `held(stale)` | operator uses `runtime stop --force` |
+| Last lease released | linger then drain | runtime group reaped, socket unlinked, lock released | n/a |
+| Broker `SIGTERM`/`SIGINT` | drain: notify, reap runtime, clean up, exit | none needed | each client receives `shutting_down`, terminates its Pi group, exits non-zero `broker_terminated` |
+| Broker `SIGKILL` while serving | runtime orphaned, socket inode left, lock released by kernel | the next broker to win the election reclaims per the section 6.2 B6 subroutine: verify pid, non-zombie, uid, start time and one of the two recorded identity shapes, then kill the group; never attach | clients see EOF, terminate Pi, exit non-zero `broker_terminated` |
+| Broker `SIGKILL` between B2 and B4 | none | the predecessor's record, untouched — B3 only reads | the next broker reclaims the predecessor exactly as this one would have. Meanwhile an operator sees `starting-unverified`, not `absent` (probe P16.A) |
+| Broker `SIGKILL` between B4 and B6, predecessor runtime still alive | the **predecessor's** runtime | own record, `stage = elected`, carrying the predecessor's block as `inherited-unreclaimed` | the inherited block is reclaimed exactly as the broker's own would be. Dropping the block instead reproduces the forbidden state (probe P17.A/P17.B) |
+| Broker `SIGKILL` between B6 and B9 | no runtime was ever created | own owner record present, `state = starting`, no runtime block | affirmative "nothing to reclaim"; the next broker proceeds and starts exactly one runtime (probe P12.B) |
+| Broker `SIGKILL` between B9 and B11 | an unauthorized launcher, which reads EOF and exits without exec'ing | owner record, with the runtime block if B10 completed | the pid is found gone ⇒ stale record, or matches the recorded `pre_exec` shape ⇒ its group is killed. No runtime ever bound anything (probe P12.C) |
+| Broker `SIGSTOP`ped or hard-blocked between B2 and B4 | none | **none** — the lock is held and no record exists | `starting-unverified`. `status` names the state and lists kernel-visible candidate holders with their `p_stat`; `stop --force` re-polls for the bounded window and then refuses `shared_runtime_owner_unidentifiable` **without signalling**, because the candidate set can hold two indistinguishable processes (probes P16.A, P16.D) |
+| Recorded runtime pid is an unreaped zombie | — | B6 reads `p_stat == SZOMB` ⇒ stale record, block dropped | a fresh runtime starts; one corpse cannot block every future start (probe P15) |
+| Recorded runtime pid is live but its argv matches neither recorded shape | — | `shared_runtime_orphan_unidentifiable` — nothing is signalled and nothing is started | refusal; operator inspects. A contradiction is reported, never guessed past (probe P13.B) |
+| Second `agents-infra runtime broker` invoked while an incumbent is **starting** | none | the candidate loses the `flock` at B2 and exits before reading, writing, killing, preflighting or binding | `broker_election_lost`; the incumbent's lock, port and startup are untouched (probe P8.D), and its bound socket inode is byte-identical afterwards (probe P20.A) |
+| A same-uid process binds the rendezvous path between the winner's stale-inode cleanup (B6) and its own bind (B15) | the winner's runtime is already live | own record, `state = serving` | the winner exits `broker_rendezvous_bind_conflict` and **never unlinks the contender**; asserted on the contender's `dev`/`ino`, not on the path existing (probe P20.B, with P20.C as the free-path control) |
+| `agents-infra runtime broker` typed by hand in a shell | none | the process is not a session leader | `broker_not_session_leader`, no side effect (probe P9.B) |
+| Broker forked, but its client dies or times out before attaching | broker reaches `serving` with zero leases | first-lease grace expires after `broker_start_timeout_seconds` from B14. This is now the **only** bound, because a client at its deadline signals nothing | broker drains, reaps the runtime, releases the lock (probe P19.D) |
+| Client reaches its deadline while the broker it forked is already `serving` a peer's lease | **none** — the client signals nothing | the client refuses `broker_start_timeout` reporting the child's state as `unknown`; lifetime stays with the leases, the first-lease grace and the operator | the peer's lease and the runtime are untouched. Revision 3 sent `SIGTERM` here and revoked the peer's lease (probes P19.A negative, P19.B) |
+| Runtime exits or crashes | broker `wait()` returns | broker sends `runtime_exited`, reaps the group, cleans up, exits | clients terminate Pi, exit non-zero `runtime_exited_early` |
+| Runtime hangs before readiness | broker startup timeout | broker terminates the group and exits | starter reports `runtime_readiness_timeout` |
+| Host reboot | all records stale | pid + start time mismatch ⇒ stale, records deleted | fresh start |
+| Rendezvous socket removed under a live broker | clients cannot connect | `broker.lock` is still held, so every broker a client offers exits `broker_election_lost` and no second runtime starts | `shared_runtime_peer_start_timeout`, naming the live broker pid read from the owner record; the operator runs `agents-infra runtime stop --force`, whose unreachable-broker algorithm is specified in section 10 and verified by probe P14 |
+| `broker-state.json` unreadable | — | `shared_runtime_state_unreadable`, no start. At B3 this is fatal **before** B4 writes, so a failed read can never destroy a predecessor's runtime block | refusal |
+| Broker exits fatally between B4 and B9 while its record carries an unreclaimed block | none created by this broker; the predecessor's may be alive | the record is **left in place**, block intact — a broker never removes a record naming a process it has not proven gone | the next broker reclaims it exactly as after a `SIGKILL` (section 6.2 fatal-exit discipline, test 12.2.33) |
+| Broker exits fatally after B9 and its own reap times out | the runtime it could not kill | the record survives **with** the runtime block | `runtime_shutdown_timeout`; the successor inherits the block and reaches its own verdict on its own evidence |
+| agents-infra upgraded mid-flight, new path (e.g. a Cellar directory behind a symlink swap) | peer's recorded exec path resolves to a different inode | gate 3 refuses | `broker_executable_identity_mismatch`, remedy is `agents-infra runtime stop` |
+| agents-infra upgraded mid-flight, in place (`rename` over the same path) | gate 3 **passes** — the stale broker's recorded path now resolves to the new inode (probe P5) | gate 3b refuses on the broker's start-time-recorded binary identity | `broker_build_identity_mismatch`, remedy is `agents-infra runtime stop` |
+| Broker exits during startup (bad profile source, port occupied, runtime start failure) | broker child terminates before binding | the client's `wait4(WNOHANG)` in step 4b observes the exit; the kernel releases `broker.lock` | `broker_start_failed` with the broker exit status and `broker.log` tail — immediate, not after the full deadline |
+| Peer's broker wedged before binding | `broker.lock` held, socket never appears | every broker this client offers exits `broker_election_lost`; this client started nothing | `shared_runtime_peer_start_timeout`, carrying this client's configured and the broker's effective timeout; operator runs `runtime stop --force` |
+| Broker drains between the client's `connect()` and a completed handshake | client sees EOF or `ECONNRESET` mid-handshake | §6.5 bounded retry, up to 3 re-entries into the step 4 loop | attaches through the next broker, or `shared_runtime_unavailable` |
+| `agents-infra runtime broker` invoked under `setsid` with a genuine key while a broker runs | none | it loses the B2 election | `broker_election_lost`; the live broker is never displaced, and nothing was read, killed, preflighted or bound |
+| `agents-infra runtime broker` invoked under `setsid` with a genuine key and **no** broker running | it wins the election and serves | this is a legitimate broker: same binary, same recomputed `runtime_key`, same gates | it starts the correct runtime; clients attest it exactly as they would any other. Section 10 states why this is admitted rather than refused |
+| `agents-infra runtime runtime-launch` invoked by hand | none | descriptor 3 is absent or not a FIFO | `runtime_launch_unauthorized` with reason `no_authorization_descriptor`, no exec, no runtime. The reason is asserted rather than the code, because the same code covers three other branches (probe P21.E) |
+| The authorization frame's member set is out of contract — an unknown member, a repeated member at any count and any position, a missing member, or bytes after the object | none | B12 step 4 compares the decoded key multiset against the compiled allowed set before comparing any value | `protocol_violation` with the reason and, where applicable, the offending member; no `execve`, never retried. A same-uid writer can mint any *value* in the frame (§9), so what this closes is not forgery but the launcher's ability to state what it received at all (probes P22.B-E) |
+| An in-place upgrade lands between a broker's own start and its B9, so the launcher is a **newer build** than the broker authorizing it | none | the frame's `schema` or `protocol_version` differs from the launcher's compiled constants | `runtime_authorization_mismatch` naming that field, no `execve`. This is the launcher-side twin of the stale-broker case gate 3b covers, and the launcher has no handshake with which to run gate 3b (§9; probes P21.B, P21.C) |
+| A same-uid process invokes `runtime-launch` with its own FIFO and mints a wholly valid frame | none | admitted, exactly as a hand-invoked `broker` is | it runs **only** what that project composes, with that argv: the frame decides *whether*, the recomposition decides *what*. §10.3 states why this pair, and not either half, is the boundary |
+| The launcher cannot arm the bound on its authorization read | none | fatal before the read | `runtime_launch_unauthorized` with reason `deadline_unavailable`, no `execve`. A launcher that cannot bound its wait refuses rather than waiting (probe P21.F) |
+| `runtime-launch`'s project directory is unreadable, or its profile no longer composes | none | fatal at B12 step 2, before the frame is read | `runtime_launch_identity_unresolvable`, no `execve`. There is no fallback target: a failed read is not an absence (probe P18.C, with the fallback mutant as the control) |
+| Project configuration edited between B5 and B12 | none | the launcher's recomputed `runtime_key` differs from `--runtime-key` | `runtime_launch_identity_mismatch`, no `execve` (probe P18.B). Because executable, argv and both runtime timeouts are inside `profile_digest`, key equality is what pins the plan |
+| Authorization frame's `exec_plan_digest` disagrees with the launcher's own recomputation | none | the durable record and the process it names would describe different plans | `runtime_authorization_mismatch` with `mismatch_field = "exec_plan_digest"`, no `execve` (probes P18.D, P21.D) |
+| Authorization frame names another pid, or another `runtime_key` | none | the frame was minted for a different claimant or a different runtime | `runtime_authorization_mismatch` naming that field, no `execve` (probes P11.C, P21.D) |
+
+Every pid-bearing decision pairs the pid with its kernel start time and argv. A
+bare pid is never acted upon, so pid reuse cannot cause a wrong kill or a wrong
+attach.
+
+## 9. Security and threat boundary
+
+In scope, and newly enforced by this specification:
+
+- broker executable identity equality with the caller's own binary, so no other
+  program can serve the rendezvous address;
+- peer uid equality on both directions of the socket;
+- exact `runtime_key`, `profile_digest`, endpoint, and runtime executable
+  `dev/ino/size/mode` equality before a lease exists;
+- kernel-sourced runtime process attestation (uid, start time, exec path, exact
+  argv) performed independently by every client;
+- exact `/v1/models` model discovery performed independently by every client;
+- pid + start time pairing on every liveness, kill, and reclamation decision;
+- broker **build** identity via the binary `dev/ino/size/mode` the broker
+  recorded at its own startup, which refuses a broker left running across an
+  in-place upgrade (gate 3b);
+- single-flight as a kernel fact the broker establishes about **itself**: it
+  holds `broker.lock` because it acquired it, not because it inherited a
+  descriptor whose holder it would have to infer;
+- a runtime that cannot come into existence before the durable record naming it,
+  and that removes itself if its broker disappears before authorizing it;
+- a launcher whose executable, argv, cwd and time bound come from its **own**
+  re-composition of the same project directory, pinned to the broker's by
+  `runtime_key` equality and cross-checked by `exec_plan_digest`, so that nothing
+  about *what to run* is carried on a channel at all;
+- an owner record published as the broker's first side effect, so ownership of
+  the lock and identifiability of the owner begin at the same step rather than
+  several steps apart;
+- liveness that requires existence **and** `p_stat != SZOMB`, everywhere;
+- anchored, `O_NOFOLLOW`, revalidated managed path handling for all new state;
+- fail-closed `*_unavailable` outcomes wherever evidence cannot be read.
+
+**Stated limits of the identity gates**, so that no later reader infers a
+guarantee that is not there:
+
+- Gate 3 compares the inode **at** the peer's recorded exec path, not the inode
+  the peer is executing. A `rename`-over upgrade therefore does not trip it
+  (probe P5); gate 3b is what refuses that case, and section 8 names gate 3b, not
+  gate 3, in that row.
+- Because gate 3b is the defence against a stale broker, **any change to broker
+  behaviour, message shape, `broker-state.json` contents, or the gate set
+  requires a protocol version bump** (gate 5). **Revision 4 is such a change and
+  bumps the version**: it adds `stage`, `runtime_key_claimed` and
+  `runtime.exec_plan_digest` to the record, adds `exec_plan_digest` to the
+  authorization frame, and adds two arguments to the `runtime-launch` argv.
+  **Revision 5 bumps it again, to `5`**, because it changes the launcher's gate
+  set; section 16.3 records why a compatibility argument does not earn an
+  exception, given that the frame's bytes did not change (§16.5 after revision 9's
+  renumbering; the pointer had drifted one section as revisions were prepended). **Revision 6 bumps it
+  to `6`** on the identical rule and for the identical reason: the frame's bytes
+  are unchanged again, and the launcher's gate set has a whole new member again.
+  **Revision 7 does not bump it**, on the same rule read the other way: it adds
+  evidence and pins two existing rules as classes, and changes no gate the
+  launcher applies, so there is no client the bump would protect.
+  Applying the rule a second time to a change that is *smaller* than the first is
+  what keeps it a rule rather than a case-by-case judgement — the moment a bump
+  is skipped because "nothing on the wire moved", the version stops meaning
+  anything a reader can rely on. A
+  revision-3 broker left running across the upgrade must be refused by gate 5,
+  not merely by gate 3b, because its record and frames no longer mean what a
+  revision-4 client reads them as. A maintenance release that changes
+  broker behaviour without bumping the version, and is installed by `rename` over
+  the same path, would otherwise be admitted whenever the inode comparison
+  happened to be satisfied. The version bump is normative, not advisory.
+- **The launcher needs the same defence, and its version skew is reachable by
+  the same upgrade.** B9 spawns "the broker's own executable" by *path*, so an
+  in-place `rename`-over upgrade landing between a broker's own start and its B9
+  produces a launcher running build N+1 authorized by a broker running build N —
+  the launcher-side twin of the stale-broker case gate 3b exists for. The
+  launcher cannot use gate 3b: it has no handshake and no announced record, only
+  five fields on a pipe. `protocol_version` equality is therefore the **only**
+  thing standing between a new-build launcher and a frame written under the old
+  build's meaning, and `schema` equality is the only thing standing between it
+  and a frame written under the old build's *shape*. Neither is redundant with
+  `runtime_key` or `exec_plan_digest`: those two would also diverge if the digest
+  *inputs* changed, but a release that changes the record layout, the frame
+  fields, the argv scrubbing rules or the gate set — every one of which section 9
+  already requires a bump for — can leave both digests identical while changing
+  what the frame means. Revision 4 asserted the version gate and shipped no
+  evidence for it; the revision-4 review minted `protocol_version = 999` with a
+  correct pid, key and digest and reached `execve`. Probe P21.B is the refusal,
+  with the delete mutant and the `>= 1` narrowing mutant both reaching `execve`
+  so the gate is proved to be an equality rather than a presence.
+- **No field the frame carries may go uncompared, and no field may go
+  uncounted.** The first half is revision 5's rule: the defect it closed was not
+  a wrong comparison but a field transmitted, parsed and then not used. The
+  second half is revision 6's, and it is the half that has teeth, because the
+  first cannot be enforced by comparing. An implementation that reads a sixth
+  field is out of contract whether or not it compares it — and an implementation
+  that *cannot tell* whether a sixth field arrived is out of contract too, since
+  it has no basis on which to make the claim. The field set is closed at the
+  decoder (B12 step 4) over the decoded key multiset; every member is an equality
+  gate (B12 step 5); adding a member is a version bump.
+
+  The general form, since this task has now met it twice from two sides: **a
+  check can only be as strong as the evidence still present when it runs.**
+  Revision 4 stated a comparison and shipped a launcher that had discarded the
+  value before making it. Revision 5 made five true comparisons after discarding
+  the knowledge that there had been six fields. Both suites were green and every
+  individual assertion in them was true.
+
+  Revision 7 adds the third side, which is about the proof rather than about the
+  launcher: **a rule stated as a class cannot be proved by a sample.** Revision
+  6's decoder was correct, but its evidence named one duplicate value pair and
+  one unknown member name, and two narrowed decoders — refuse a duplicate only
+  when the values differ, refuse only the one sampled unknown name — satisfy
+  every row of it while admitting frames this document refuses. The consequence
+  for anything implemented against this section: where a rule quantifies over a
+  **finite closed** set, prove it over the whole set; where it quantifies over an
+  **open** one, prove it by near-miss classes, each paired with a narrowing that
+  only that class defeats. A deletion proves a clause is present. Only a
+  narrowing proves what the clause covers.
+- Revision 2 asked `flock` a question it cannot answer — whether *this* process
+  holds an inherited lock — and the second review demonstrated the consequence:
+  during a peer's startup, a descriptor open on the correct `broker.lock` inode
+  but not holding it passed the gate, because the incumbent's lock supplied the
+  `EWOULDBLOCK`, while the runtime port and the rendezvous path were both still
+  free. Revision 3 does not answer that question better; it stops asking it. The
+  broker acquires the lock in its own process (section 6.2, B2) and the client
+  never opens the file at all. The attack shape is refused on the lock alone,
+  with a discriminating control (probe P8.D/P8.E).
+- The authorization frame handed to the launcher is **not** a secret and is not
+  claimed to be unforgeable. Same-uid forgery is out of the threat model. What
+  the launcher gate closes is an ad-hoc invocation (descriptor 3 absent or not a
+  FIFO), a frame naming another process, and — the case it exists for — a broker
+  that died before authorizing, which the launcher observes as EOF and answers by
+  removing itself (probe P11).
+- **The elected-owner window is narrowed, not closed.** `broker.lock` is acquired
+  at B2 and the owner record is published at B4, and no atomic operation spans
+  both. A broker `SIGSTOP`ped or hard-blocked in between holds the lock with no
+  record, and probe P16.A produces exactly that state. Revision 3 asserted the
+  state was unreachable and refused on that basis; the assertion was false, and a
+  false impossibility claim is worse than a named limitation because it stops
+  anyone from handling the case. Revision 4 states the limitation, reports the
+  state as `starting-unverified` with kernel-visible candidates and their
+  `p_stat`, and still refuses to signal — because probe P16.D shows two
+  candidates that are indistinguishable on uid, binary inode, argv and `p_stat`
+  while only one holds the lock. Identifying a lock holder is not possible with
+  `flock` on this platform (rev-1 reviewer probe: `F_GETLK` cannot see a BSD
+  `flock`), and no amount of process enumeration converts a set into a proof.
+- **`exec_plan_digest` is a consistency check, not an authorization.** It is
+  computed independently on both sides from the same configuration, so an
+  attacker who can write the frame can also write a matching digest. What it
+  actually catches is an implementation in which the durable record and the
+  launcher's own composition have diverged — the record naming plan X while the
+  process runs plan Y — which is the shape that would make every later
+  reclamation decision wrong. It is claimed as nothing more.
+- `broker_not_session_leader` proves detachment, not provenance. A process
+  deliberately detached with `setsid(1)` and given a genuine `--runtime-key`
+  passes it, and that is correct: it is the same binary recomputing the same
+  digest from the same project, which is what every other gate already requires.
+  The gate refuses the accidental case — a broker typed into a shell, sharing
+  that shell's session and dying with it.
+
+Explicitly out of the threat model, unchanged from cycle 8:
+
+- a malicious or compromised `runtime.executable` selected by reviewed project
+  policy, including one that proxies or lies about its own capabilities;
+- a malicious same-uid process that wins the bind race in the window between the
+  broker's preflight close and the runtime's own `bind`. The runtime cannot bind
+  in that case and exits, and the broker refuses on direct-child exit, so the
+  practical outcome is a refusal rather than a silent attach — but this is a
+  consequence of runtime behavior, not a guarantee agents-infra enforces;
+- a same-uid process forging `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` to collide with another run's
+  client state;
+- `ptrace`, code injection, or any same-uid tampering with a live broker;
+- compromised kernel or platform libraries.
+
+**Socket-ownership attestation is deliberately not claimed.** Proving that the
+listener on `127.0.0.1:<port>` is held by the attested runtime pid requires
+`proc_pidfdinfo`/`PROC_PIDFDSOCKETINFO`, which needs cgo or a raw `proc_info`
+syscall, and it would close only the bind-race window that cycle 8 already
+documents as out of scope. Adding it would change build constraints for one
+already-accepted residual risk. It is recorded here as a future hardening with a
+typed `runtime_listener_attestation_unavailable` reserved for it; until it
+exists, no code and no diagnostic may claim listener ownership.
+
+Diagnostics never print secrets, `--api-key` values, arbitrary environment
+values, or raw model output. They print managed paths, hashes, pids, start
+times, exec paths, and the exact configured argv, all of which the caller's own
+configuration already contains.
+
+## 10. CLI surfaces
+
+`agents-infra runtime` is a **new top-level subcommand**. It cannot live under
+`agents-infra pi`, because the Pi wrapper forwards every non-flag token to Pi as
+a message operand, so `agents-infra pi runtime status` would be sent to the
+model rather than parsed.
+
+```
+agents-infra runtime status [--project DIR] [--profile NAME] [--json]
+agents-infra runtime stop   [--project DIR] [--profile NAME] [--force] [--timeout SECONDS]
+agents-infra runtime broker         --runtime-key HEX --profile-project DIR --profile NAME  (internal)
+agents-infra runtime runtime-launch --runtime-key HEX --profile-project DIR --profile NAME  (internal)
+```
+
+### 10.1 `status`
+
+`status` never starts anything. It reports, per resolved profile: sharing mode;
+`broker.state` in `absent | starting-unverified | starting | serving | lingering
+| draining | unverified-stale`; broker pid, start time, uptime; runtime pid, pgid, start time,
+exec path, argv, cwd; endpoint; `runtime_key` and `profile_digest`; the lease
+table with `lease_id`, `run_id`, client pid, age, heartbeat age, and lease state;
+the outcome of every section 7.2 gate it evaluated; and `sharing.effective` —
+with `sharing.configured` printed beside it, plus the broker pid and start time
+that fixed the effective values, whenever the resolved profile's own sharing
+policy differs (section 5.4).
+
+`status` prefers the connected view: it attaches, runs the section 7.2
+handshake, and sends `status`. When no broker answers — which includes the whole
+`starting` state, since the socket is bound only at B15 — it falls back to the
+owner record on disk and reports every field it derives from it as
+`record-derived`, never as attested. Because the owner record now exists from B4,
+the broker's *first* side effect, `status` can report a broker that is still
+bringing a 27B model up, with `broker.state = starting`, `stage = elected` or
+`composed`, and no runtime block, instead of the `absent` that revision 2 would
+have printed for tens of seconds. A record it cannot read is reported
+`unreadable`, never `absent`. It distinguishes `absent` from `unreadable` in
+every field and marks mirror-derived data `unverified`.
+
+**`starting-unverified`.** When no broker answers *and* no owner record can be
+read, `status` does one further thing before it reports `absent`: it opens
+`broker.lock` and attempts `flock(LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB)` on its own descriptor.
+
+- Acquired ⇒ nothing owns this runtime. Release immediately and report `absent`.
+- `EWOULDBLOCK` ⇒ some process holds the lock and has published no record. Report
+  `broker.state = starting-unverified` — never `absent` — and, because an
+  operator staring at that word needs somewhere to go, enumerate the
+  kernel-visible **candidate holders**: same effective uid, not a zombie,
+  executing the same binary `dev`/`ino` as `status` itself, and carrying an argv
+  that is an `agents-infra runtime broker` invocation for this `runtime_key`.
+  For each, print pid, `p_stat` decoded (`SRUN`, `SSLEEP`, `SSTOP`), start time
+  and exact argv. A `SIGSTOP`ped broker therefore appears as a stopped candidate
+  rather than as nothing at all (probe P16.A/P16.B).
+- Any other error on the lock ⇒ `shared_runtime_state_path_invalid`.
+
+The candidate list is **reporting, and is labelled as such**. It is never an
+input to any decision, here or in `stop`. Probe P16.C shows the filter
+discriminates — a same-uid process holding the same lock with a foreign argv is
+excluded — and probe P16.D shows why that is still not enough to act on: two
+frozen candidates with identical uid, binary inode, argv and `p_stat` can exist
+while exactly one of them holds the lock. An empty candidate list under a held
+lock is reported as such and means the holder is not a broker of this build.
+
+Exit `0` when the state was determined, non-zero when it could not be read.
+`starting-unverified` is a determined state and exits `0`.
+
+### 10.2 `stop`
+
+`stop` has two paths. Revision 3 first specified the second one, which review
+`RUN-260825-ac68af` had found named as a remedy in section 8 and never defined.
+Revision 4 corrects that specification where the third review
+(`RUN-260825-969723`, finding F3) showed it asserted an impossibility instead of
+handling a reachable state.
+
+**Connected path.** `connect` the rendezvous socket and run the section 7.2
+handshake — a `stop` request is attested exactly like an `acquire`, so an
+unattested peer cannot ask a broker to shut down. Then send `stop`.
+
+- with live leases and without `--force` ⇒ `shared_runtime_leases_active`, list
+  them, exit non-zero;
+- with `--force` ⇒ the broker revokes leases, notifies clients, and drains;
+- wait up to `--timeout` for the endpoint to become free; exit non-zero if it
+  does not.
+
+**Unreachable path**, taken when `connect` yields `ENOENT`/`ECONNREFUSED`, or
+when a socket exists but no handshake completes. This covers the section 8 row
+"rendezvous socket removed under a live broker", where the broker is alive, holds
+the lock, and is **not** an orphan. Without `--force` it refuses
+`shared_runtime_broker_unreachable_use_force` and signals nothing. With
+`--force`, in order:
+
+1. Read `broker-state.json`. Unreadable, partial or malformed ⇒
+   `shared_runtime_state_unreadable`, **no signal**, exit non-zero. A failed read
+   is never an absence.
+2. Absent ⇒ take `broker.lock` with `LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB`.
+   - Acquired ⇒ nothing is running. Under the lock, remove stale `leases/`
+     entries and any stale rendezvous inode, report `absent`, exit `0`.
+   - `EWOULDBLOCK` ⇒ `starting-unverified`. Some process holds the lock and has
+     published no record.
+
+     Revision 3 said this state "no broker of this protocol version can produce"
+     and refused on that basis. Review finding F3 showed the claim is false: B2
+     takes the lock and B4 publishes, and a broker stopped or hard-blocked in
+     between produces it exactly (probe P16.A). Revision 4 replaces the false
+     impossibility with a **bounded re-poll**, because both benign resolutions
+     are self-resolving and neither needs a signal:
+
+     a. Re-poll, at 100ms, until `--timeout` elapses, for either the owner record
+        becoming readable — in which case restart this algorithm at step 1, which
+        lands on the identified path at step 3 — or the lock becoming acquirable,
+        in which case take the `absent` branch above. Probe P16.A verifies the
+        first: after `SIGCONT` the record appears with the lock still held.
+     b. If neither happens within `--timeout`, report
+        `shared_runtime_owner_unidentifiable`, **signal nothing**, exit non-zero,
+        and print the diagnosis rather than a shrug: a lock held this long with
+        no record means the holder is job-control stopped, blocked on storage
+        that cannot be read, or not a broker of this build at all. Print the same
+        candidate-holder list section 10.1 defines, with each candidate's
+        `p_stat`, and name the operator's own next step (`lsof` on the lock path,
+        then a decision only a human can make).
+
+     `stop --force` still does not pick a candidate to signal, and this is the
+     one place the restraint costs something, so the reason is stated plainly:
+     probe P16.D produces two candidates that are byte-identical on uid, binary
+     inode, argv and `p_stat` while exactly one holds the lock. `flock` cannot
+     name its holder on this platform, so a set is all there is, and signalling
+     one member of a set is guessing. An operator with `lsof` can do better than
+     agents-infra can, and is told so.
+3. Present ⇒ verify the **broker** from the kernel, not from the record's own
+   say-so: the pid exists and `p_stat != SZOMB`; `uid` equals the caller's
+   effective uid; kernel start time byte-equal to the record; exec path resolving
+   to the same `dev`/`ino` as the caller's own executable; exact argv byte-equal
+   to the recorded broker argv. Any mismatch or unreadable field ⇒
+   `broker_stop_identity_mismatch`, **no signal**, exit non-zero. Probe P14.A:
+   a record whose broker start time has been altered leaves the live broker
+   untouched.
+
+   A record at `stage = "elected"` — published at B4, before B5 verified the
+   key — is stoppable through this path, and that is correct rather than a gap.
+   Every field this step checks is kernel-sourced and complete from B4, and the
+   record was read from a directory whose path is already derived from the
+   `runtime_key` the operator resolved, so an `elected`-stage record cannot be
+   found under another runtime's key. What is unverified at that stage is which
+   *profile* the broker will serve, which this step never consults.
+4. Verified ⇒ `SIGTERM` the broker pid, wait up to `--timeout`, escalate to
+   `SIGKILL`, and require the pid to become not-live under the corrected
+   predicate. Failure ⇒ `runtime_shutdown_timeout`, exit non-zero.
+5. The dead broker has released `broker.lock`. Acquire it, then run the **same**
+   reclamation subroutine as section 6.2 B6 against the record's runtime block,
+   with the same five refusals and the same two admissible identity shapes. This
+   is deliberately one subroutine and not two: an operator path that reclaimed on
+   weaker evidence than a broker does would be the bypass around the check.
+6. Remove `leases/`, `broker-state.json` and the rendezvous inode; report the
+   broker pid, the runtime pid, and what was terminated. This step obeys the same
+   ordering as the section 6.2 fatal-exit discipline: the record is removed only
+   after step 5 proved the runtime gone, and a step-5 refusal
+   (`shared_runtime_orphan_unidentifiable`, `runtime_shutdown_timeout`) leaves the
+   record and its runtime block in place. An operator path that forgot a runtime
+   it could not kill would be strictly worse than a broker doing the same.
+
+Verified end to end by probe P14.B — a live broker with no socket is identified
+from the owner record, stopped, its lock released, and its runtime reclaimed —
+with P14.C as the empty-state control and P14.A as the refusal.
+
+### 10.3 `broker` and `runtime-launch`
+
+Both are internal entry points, and revision 3 states plainly what that does and
+does not mean.
+
+`broker` refuses two things about itself, in this order: it must be a session
+leader (`broker_not_session_leader`, probe P9), and its recomputed `runtime_key`
+must equal `--runtime-key`, where the recomputation re-runs the **full profile
+composition** from `--profile-project DIR`. `--profile-project` is a project
+directory and not a single config file precisely because a profile composed
+across several files cannot be reproduced from one path, and the
+self-verification is fatal on mismatch. The source is read exactly once, at
+startup — twice per runtime overall, since `runtime-launch` composes
+independently, which is the point rather than a redundancy.
+
+What `broker` does **not** do is refuse a deliberate invocation. A process
+detached with `setsid` and given a genuine `--runtime-key` for a real project's
+real profile is admitted, and that is the honest outcome: it is the same
+executable, recomputing the same digest from the same configuration, subject to
+the same election and the same gates. Refusing it would be ceremony, and revision
+2's attempt to refuse it was the mechanism the second review broke. What must be
+refused — a different binary, a repointed key, a divergent profile, a second
+broker while one is running — is refused by gate 3, by the `runtime_key`
+recomputation, by gate 7, and by the B2 election respectively, each separately
+testable (section 12.2.18, 12.2.22) and none doing another's work.
+
+`runtime-launch` takes the **same three arguments as `broker`**, and that is
+revision 4's answer to review finding F1. Revision 3 passed it only
+`--runtime-key` while requiring it to apply `runtime.startup_timeout_seconds` and
+to `execve` `runtime.executable + runtime.argv`. Nothing supplied those values and
+a SHA-256 cannot be inverted, so the entry point was not implementable from its
+declared inputs; the revision-3 probes only appeared to work because their
+launcher took the target, its argv and its timeout from `PROBE_TARGET`,
+`PROBE_TARGET_ARG` and `PROBE_AUTH_TIMEOUT_MS`, a probe-only channel with no
+normative counterpart. That is the "capability claim that does not reproduce"
+shape, and the review was right to block on it.
+
+The fix does not add a channel. `runtime-launch` is the same binary as the
+broker, so it re-runs the same full composition from the same project directory
+and derives the executable, the exact argv, the time bound and the expected
+`exec_plan_digest` itself (section 6.2, B12 step 2). The binding to the broker's
+composition is `runtime_key` equality: section 5.1 puts `runtime.executable`,
+`runtime.argc`, every `runtime.argv[i]` and both runtime timeouts inside
+`profile_digest`, and `runtime_key` covers `profile_digest`, so two processes that
+compute the same key necessarily hold the same plan. Probe P18.A drives the real
+entry point end to end and reads the resulting exec path and exact argv back from
+`kern.procargs2` on the same pid.
+
+**Why the plan is not simply put in the authorization frame.** It would work, and
+it is rejected: a frame carrying the executable and argv makes the pipe a command
+channel, so whoever obtains the write end chooses what runs. Under recomposition
+the write end chooses only *whether* something runs, and *what* is fixed by
+configuration both processes read independently. The same-uid threat boundary
+does not make the distinction moot — it makes it cheap, since keeping the plan off
+the channel costs one composition and buys a smaller thing to get wrong. Section
+14 records the rejection.
+
+`runtime-launch` never runs a runtime on its own authority. Invoked by hand it
+finds no FIFO on descriptor 3 and exits `runtime_launch_unauthorized` before
+touching anything. Given a FIFO but an unreadable or non-composing project, it
+exits `runtime_launch_identity_unresolvable` **without** `execve` and without any
+fallback target — probe P18.C pairs that refusal with a mutant that treats the
+failed read as satisfied and does exec, so the refusal is a distinction rather
+than a launcher that never runs anything.
+
+Every `runtime-launch` refusal is typed **and carries the fact that produced
+it**: a `reason` for `runtime_launch_unauthorized`, which is one code covering
+`no_authorization_descriptor`, `deadline_unavailable`,
+`broker_died_before_authorizing` and `authorization_deadline`; and a
+`mismatch_field` for `runtime_authorization_mismatch`, which is one code covering
+the five frame comparisons; and a `reason` plus, where a single member is at
+fault, a `mismatch_field` for `protocol_violation`, which since revision 6 is one
+code covering the five ways a frame's *shape* can be out of contract. This is not
+diagnostics polish. Revision 4's test
+12.2.18b claimed the absent-FIFO case refused, but asserted only an exit code
+that the EOF branch produces as well — so the claim was not earned by the
+evidence attached to it, which is the same shape as the finding the revision-4
+review raised. Probe P21.E holds the four unauthorized shapes apart, and its
+`collapse_fd3` mutant reproduces revision 4: with the descriptor gate deleted, an
+absent descriptor still exits with the same code while attributing the refusal
+to a broker that never existed. Probe P22 does the same for the shape reasons —
+`frame_unknown_field` and `frame_duplicate_field` are what let test 12.2.30c say
+which clause refused, and a launcher that reported a bare `protocol_violation`
+would be indistinguishable from one refusing every frame it cannot parse.
+
+What `runtime-launch` deliberately does **not** refuse, for the same reason
+`broker` does not, is a hand-invoked launcher given a genuine project and a
+self-minted but wholly valid frame. It runs exactly the runtime that project
+composes, under the same argv, and nothing else: recomposition is what makes the
+concession bounded. The frame gates constrain *whose* authorization is accepted
+and under *which* protocol; the composition constrains *what* can run. Only the
+pair is a boundary.
+
+`agents-infra pi --print-config` gains a `shared_runtime` block reporting mode,
+`runtime_key`, `profile_digest`, rendezvous and state paths, and the
+**configured** sharing policy fields, with `broker.observed = "not-inspected"`
+and no effective-policy field at all, because determining the effective policy
+would require connecting. It performs no connect, bind, socket creation, or
+process inspection, preserving the cycle-8 non-launching guarantee.
+
+Traceability: `STORY-260825-1r7z9o` AC "status, explicit stop"; the DoD item
+"terminal-independent broker ownership and IPC boundaries".
+
+## 11. Typed refusals
+
+New codes, added to the existing Pi failure table. Every one of them means no
+lease was granted.
+
+| Code | Condition | Managed processes started? |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `shared_runtime_state_path_invalid` | New managed path cannot be resolved, contained, opened without following a symlink, or revalidated | No |
+| `shared_runtime_state_unreadable` | `broker-state.json` present but unreadable, partial, or malformed. Never degraded to "absent", on any path, including `stop --force` | No |
+| `broker_rendezvous_path_too_long` | Composed socket path exceeds platform `sun_path` capacity | No |
+| `broker_unreachable` | `connect` fails with anything other than `ENOENT`/`ECONNREFUSED` | No |
+| `broker_start_timeout` | No broker became connectable within this client's deadline. Carries the broker child's pid and raw `wait4(WNOHANG)` result, and reports the broker's state as **`unknown`** — never `starting`, which is the inference review finding F2 struck down | **No.** The client signals nothing at its deadline: it cannot distinguish a broker that is still starting from one already serving a peer's lease, and both present the identical `wait4` answer (probes P19.A, P19.B). Lifetime stays with the leases, the first-lease grace and the operator |
+| `shared_runtime_peer_start_timeout` | Every broker this client offered exited `EXIT_ELECTION_LOST` and the peer's broker never became connectable within `broker_start_timeout_seconds`. Carries this client's configured and the broker's effective timeout (section 5.4) | No — this client started nothing, and must not |
+| `broker_start_failed` | This client's own broker child exited during startup with a status other than `EXIT_ELECTION_LOST`; carries the exit status and the `broker.log` tail | The broker exited on its own; its runtime group, if any, is reaped by the broker before exit |
+| `broker_election_lost` | A broker lost the B2 `flock` race. Delivered as the broker's exit status `EXIT_ELECTION_LOST`, **not** surfaced to the user as a failure: the client records it and keeps attaching | No — nothing read, killed, preflighted or bound |
+| `broker_not_session_leader` | The broker entry point was invoked by a process that is not its own session leader, i.e. by hand from a shell rather than through the section 6.3 fork | No |
+| `runtime_launch_unauthorized` | `runtime-launch` found no FIFO on descriptor 3, could not arm the bound on its authorization read, or its broker died or timed out before authorizing it. The process exits without `execve`. The payload carries a **`reason`** — `no_authorization_descriptor`, `deadline_unavailable`, `broker_died_before_authorizing`, `authorization_deadline` — because one code covering four branches that a test cannot tell apart proves none of them (probes P21.E, P21.F) | No — no runtime ever exists |
+| `runtime_launch_identity_unresolvable` | `runtime-launch` could not compose its profile: `--profile-project`/`--profile` missing, or the project unreadable, unparseable, or no longer defining that profile. **No fallback target exists**, and a failed read is never treated as an absence | No — no `execve` (probe P18.C, with the fallback mutant as the discriminating control) |
+| `runtime_launch_identity_mismatch` | `runtime-launch`'s recomputed `runtime_key` differs from `--runtime-key`, i.e. the project's configuration no longer composes to the runtime the broker is starting | No — no `execve` (probe P18.B) |
+| `runtime_authorization_mismatch` | Any field of the authorization frame differs from the launcher's own value: a foreign `schema`, another `protocol_version`, another pid, another `runtime_key`, or an `exec_plan_digest` the launcher's own composition does not produce. The payload carries **`mismatch_field`** naming which of the five diverged; comparisons are equalities, never compatibility ranges. Reached only for frames whose shape B12 step 4 already accepted — a frame out of contract as to its *members* refuses as `protocol_violation` instead, and the distinction is normative because the two codes name faults in different parties | No (probes P11.C, P18.D, P21.B, P21.C, P21.D) |
+| `shared_runtime_orphan_unidentifiable` | The recorded runtime pid is live with a byte-equal start time, but its uid or its exec path and argv match neither the recorded pre-exec nor the recorded post-exec identity shape | No — nothing is signalled and nothing is started |
+| `shared_runtime_owner_unidentifiable` | `stop --force` found `broker.lock` held with no readable `broker-state.json` and neither condition resolved within `--timeout` — the `starting-unverified` state. Reachable: a broker `SIGSTOP`ped or hard-blocked between B2 and B4 produces it (probe P16.A). Carries the candidate-holder list with each `p_stat` | No — nothing is signalled. The candidate set can hold two indistinguishable members while one holds the lock (probe P16.D) |
+| `broker_stop_identity_mismatch` | `stop --force` could not verify the recorded broker against the kernel (pid gone, zombie, uid, start time, exec identity or argv) | No — the live process is left untouched |
+| `shared_runtime_broker_unreachable_use_force` | `stop` without `--force` found no reachable broker but state that may still exist | No |
+| `shared_runtime_not_configured` | `runtime status`/`stop` invoked for a profile whose `mode` is not `"shared"` | No |
+| `broker_rendezvous_bind_conflict` | Broker's rendezvous `bind` at B15 found a live listener or a stale inode at the path; only a broker holding `broker.lock` may unlink one, and only at B6 | No — the contender is never displaced, asserted on its `dev`/`ino` rather than on the path existing (probe P20.B) |
+| `broker_peer_untrusted` | Peer uid differs from the caller's effective uid | No |
+| `broker_identity_unavailable` | Peer pid, start time, exec path, or argv cannot be read | No |
+| `broker_identity_mismatch` | Announced broker start time differs from the kernel value | No |
+| `broker_executable_identity_mismatch` | Peer executable `dev`/`ino` differs from the caller's own | No |
+| `broker_build_identity_mismatch` | The binary identity the broker recorded at its own startup differs from the caller's current binary — an in-place upgrade left a stale broker running | No |
+| `broker_protocol_version_mismatch` | Announced protocol version differs | No |
+| `broker_terminated` | Broker drained or died while a lease was held | Client terminates its Pi group |
+| `shared_runtime_identity_mismatch` | Announced `runtime_key` differs | No |
+| `shared_runtime_profile_mismatch` | Announced `profile_digest` differs | No |
+| `shared_runtime_endpoint_mismatch` | Announced endpoint differs from configured `base_url` | No |
+| `shared_runtime_lease_limit` | Grant would exceed the **effective** `max_leases`; payload carries effective and configured values with the broker pid and start time | No |
+| `shared_runtime_shutting_down` | Broker is draining | No |
+| `shared_runtime_unavailable` | Bounded retries exhausted after repeated `shared_runtime_shutting_down` or mid-handshake EOF | No |
+| `shared_runtime_leases_active` | `stop` without `--force` while leases are held | No |
+| `runtime_identity_mismatch` | Kernel-observed runtime uid, start time, exec path, or argv differs from the configured or announced value | No |
+| `protocol_violation` | Oversized or malformed frame, or an authorization frame whose **shape** is out of contract: not exactly one JSON object, content after the object, or a decoded member-name multiset that is not **equal** to the five allowed members once each. Carries a **`reason`** — `frame_not_single_object`, `frame_unknown_field`, `frame_duplicate_field`, `frame_missing_field`, `frame_unparseable` — and, for the three member-level reasons, `mismatch_field` naming the offending member. Those five reasons **classify how the multiset differs**; the decision is the equality and is never reconstructed from them (§6.2 B12 step 4). Evaluated at B12 step 4, **before any value comparison** and long before `execve`: no comparison can establish that a field set was closed, because the decode that precedes it has already discarded the evidence (probes P22.B-F, P23.A-C) | No |
+
+Reused unchanged: `runtime_listener_occupied`, `runtime_listener_check_failed`,
+`runtime_executable_not_found`, `runtime_executable_invalid`,
+`runtime_start_failed`, `runtime_exited_early`, `runtime_readiness_timeout`,
+`runtime_readiness_invalid`, `runtime_model_unavailable`,
+`runtime_shutdown_timeout`, `pi_profile_busy`, and every
+`pi_execution_identity_*` code.
+
+Reserved and deliberately unimplemented:
+`runtime_listener_attestation_unavailable` (section 9).
+
+No shared-mode failure falls back to native Pi, another profile, another port,
+another runtime, an unattested listener, or an orphaned runtime.
+
+## 12. Test plan
+
+Positive tests prove reachability. The gates are proved by negatives and by
+narrowing mutants, per `references/negative-evidence.md`.
+
+### 12.1 Production-path positives
+
+1. One shared client starts a broker, gets a lease, runs Pi against a recording
+   fake runtime, exits; runtime group is reaped, socket unlinked, port free.
+2. **Two clients concurrent across the startup window.** Two clients produce
+   exactly **one** runtime spawn (counted by the recording fake) and two leases.
+
+   The startup window must be **deterministic, not incidental**. The fake
+   runtime holds `/v1/models` unready until the test releases it, and the second
+   client is started while the first client's broker is still waiting on it —
+   verified by asserting that at the moment client B enters its wait loop the
+   rendezvous socket does not exist and B's own first broker child has exited
+   `EXIT_ELECTION_LOST`. Only then is readiness released. Revision 1 of this specification failed exactly this
+   scenario (section 2, probe P7): a test that lets the first broker finish
+   binding before the second client starts passes against a broken
+   implementation, because it never enters the window. The assertion set is:
+   one runtime spawn, two leases, B's outcome is a granted lease and **not**
+   `broker_start_timeout` or `shared_runtime_peer_start_timeout`, and B's
+   attach happened after the readiness release.
+3. **Election under a real race.** N clients (N ≥ 8) start simultaneously with
+   no broker present: exactly **one** runtime spawn, N leases, exactly one broker
+   process observed acquiring `broker.lock`, and every other broker process
+   observed exiting `EXIT_ELECTION_LOST` having created nothing.
+4. **Waiter promotion, early and late.** (a) The winning broker is killed before
+   it binds; a waiting client's next broker attempt must win the freed lock and
+   the client must obtain a lease within the deadline rather than failing
+   `shared_runtime_peer_start_timeout`. (b) The same, but the incumbent is killed
+   **late** in the window, after the waiter's `election_backoff` has already
+   grown to its 30-second cap: the waiter must observe the dead broker pid in
+   `broker-state.json`, reset its backoff, and still obtain a lease before the
+   deadline. Variant (b) is the one a fixed attempt cap would fail. Both must
+   assert that promotion used no dedicated code path — only the ordinary attempt
+   after `election_backoff`.
+5. **Publish-before-run, driven through the production broker.** For each of the
+   instants B2-B4, B4-B6, B6-B9, B9-B11 and post-B11, `SIGKILL` the broker and
+   assert the section 6.4 row: no runtime process that no record names, the launcher gone,
+   exactly one replacement runtime started by the next broker, and the endpoint
+   free afterwards. The B9-B11 variant must additionally assert that the launcher
+   process **never** appeared with the runtime's exec path. The **B4-B6 variant
+   is new in revision 4 and is the one that can regress silently**: it must be run
+   with a predecessor runtime already alive, and must assert that the successor's
+   own record still names it as `inherited-unreclaimed` and that the next broker
+   kills it. A build whose B4 drops the block passes every other row in this test
+   and fails only this one (probe P17.A/P17.B).
+6. **First-lease grace.** A client forks a broker and is killed before
+   attaching. The broker must reach `serving`, grant no lease, and drain within
+   `broker_start_timeout_seconds` of B14, reaping the runtime and releasing the
+   lock. Since revision 4 this is the **only** bound on such a broker, so the test
+   must also assert that no client signalled it (probe P19.D).
+7. Client A releases while B holds: the runtime pid is unchanged and B's
+   requests still succeed. Asserts no premature shutdown.
+8. `mode` absent ⇒ the exclusive path runs and no broker, socket, or
+   `pi-runtimes/` state is created at all.
+9. `status` on a live broker reports both leases, the runtime pid, and gate
+   outcomes; `stop` refuses with `shared_runtime_leases_active`, then
+   `stop --force` drains within the timeout.
+
+### 12.2 Negatives that must fail the gate
+
+Each drives the real `RunPi` entry point.
+
+1. **Attractive foreign listener.** A plain HTTP server on the port serves a
+   perfectly valid `/v1/models` containing the exact expected model id, with no
+   rendezvous socket. Must refuse `runtime_listener_occupied`. A correct-looking
+   readiness body must never admit a listener.
+2. **Protocol-correct squatter.** A different executable binds the rendezvous
+   path and answers the handshake with a self-consistent forged record naming
+   real live pids. Must refuse `broker_executable_identity_mismatch`.
+3. **Forged runtime pid.** A test broker built from the same binary announces a
+   `runtime_pid` belonging to an unrelated live same-uid process. The client's
+   independent `kern.procargs2` read must refuse `runtime_identity_mismatch`.
+4. **Recycled pid.** Announce a pid that is alive but whose start time differs
+   from the record. Must refuse `runtime_identity_mismatch`.
+5. **Profile divergence table.** Client and broker differ in exactly one field
+   at a time: `model`, `provider`, `base_url` port, `api`, `readiness_path`,
+   each `compat` member, one `runtime.argv` token, argv token count, argv token
+   order, `runtime.executable` path, and presence/absence of `runtime.dflash`.
+   Every row must refuse `shared_runtime_profile_mismatch`.
+6. **Executable swapped after broker start.** Replace `runtime.executable` with
+   a byte-different file at the same path; the attaching client must refuse
+   `runtime_executable_invalid`.
+7. **Wrong uid.** A broker socket owned by another uid must refuse
+   `broker_peer_untrusted`. (Exercised with a fake `LOCAL_PEERCRED` reader in
+   the unit layer and skipped-with-explicit-reason where a second uid is
+   unavailable — a skip is reported, never counted as a pass.)
+8. **Unreadable evidence.** Force `kern.proc.pid` / `kern.procargs2` to fail.
+   Must produce `broker_identity_unavailable`, and must **not** start a second
+   runtime and must **not** treat the failure as absence.
+9. **Corrupt `broker-state.json`.** Truncated and malformed variants must
+   produce `shared_runtime_state_unreadable` with no runtime start.
+10. **Orphan after broker `SIGKILL`.** Kill the broker with `SIGKILL`, leave the
+    runtime alive, then run a new client. It must reclaim (kill) the orphan and
+    start a fresh runtime with a new pid, and must never attach to the orphan.
+11. **Lease limit.** `max_leases = 1`; the second client must refuse
+    `shared_runtime_lease_limit`.
+12. **Draining race.** Attach exactly while the broker drains; must observe
+    `shared_runtime_shutting_down` and then succeed through a fresh broker
+    within the bounded retry budget.
+13. **Same RUN id twice.** Two clients with the same `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` ⇒ the
+    second refuses `pi_profile_busy`.
+14. **Two default clients.** No `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` ⇒ the second refuses
+    `pi_profile_busy`, preserving today's behavior.
+15. **Cross-project, divergent profile, same port.** Must refuse
+    `shared_runtime_profile_mismatch`, not attach.
+16. **Cross-project, byte-identical profile.** Must share one runtime — the
+    positive counterpart that proves the project key is deliberately excluded
+    from `runtime_key`.
+17. **Socket path overflow.** A cache root long enough to overflow `sun_path`
+    must refuse `broker_rendezvous_path_too_long` with no side effect.
+18. **Ad-hoc broker invocation.** Launch the internal `runtime broker` entry
+    point directly. (a) From a shell, sharing the shell's session ⇒
+    `broker_not_session_leader`, no runtime, no socket, no port taken. (b) Under
+    `setsid` with a `--runtime-key` that does not match the recomputed
+    composition ⇒ fatal identity mismatch before any side effect. (c) Under
+    `setsid` with a genuine key while a broker is running ⇒
+    `broker_election_lost`, and the test must assert the incumbent's lock is
+    still held, its socket still present, and its leases intact. The control —
+    under `setsid`, genuine key, no incumbent — must **succeed**, so the tests
+    prove the gates discriminate rather than that the entry point always
+    refuses. Shapes (a) and (c) reproduce at the syscall layer as probes P9.B
+    and P8.D, with controls P9.A and P8.E.
+18a. **The revision-2 bypass shape, at the production entry point.** With an
+    incumbent broker in `starting` — lock held, rendezvous socket absent, runtime
+    port free — invoke a second broker with a descriptor open on the correct
+    `broker.lock` inode but not holding it. It must exit `broker_election_lost`
+    with no runtime spawn, no rendezvous bind, no port taken, and no disturbance
+    to the incumbent. This is the exact shape the second review demonstrated
+    against revision 2 (probe P8.D); it exists as a regression test so the
+    inherited-lock design cannot return unnoticed.
+18b. **Ad-hoc `runtime-launch` invocation.** Invoke the internal entry point with
+    (a) no descriptor 3, (b) descriptor 3 open on a regular file rather than a
+    FIFO. Both must exit `runtime_launch_unauthorized` without `execve`, and —
+    new in revision 5 — the assertion is on the refusal **reason**
+    `no_authorization_descriptor`, not on the exit code alone. The exit code is
+    shared with the EOF and deadline branches, so an exit-code assertion is
+    satisfied by a launcher that never looks at descriptor 3 at all; the
+    narrowing mutant is exactly that launcher and must redden this test. The
+    control — a FIFO carrying a valid frame naming this pid — must exec, and a
+    frame naming a **different** pid must exit `runtime_authorization_mismatch`
+    with `mismatch_field = "launcher_pid"` (probes P11.C, P21.E).
+18c. **Broker dies before authorizing.** Drive the production broker to B9, then
+    `SIGKILL` it. The launcher must exit `runtime_launch_unauthorized` and the
+    test must assert, by polling `kern.procargs2` on that pid until it is gone,
+    that it **never** carried the runtime's exec path. Without this assertion
+    "the runtime is not running now" is compatible with "the runtime ran
+    briefly", which is the state finding F1 was about.
+19. **EOF mid-handshake.** A broker accepts the connection and closes it before
+    sending `hello_ok`. The client must re-enter the §6.5 retry within its
+    retry budget and succeed against a fresh broker; it must **not** report
+    `broker_unreachable` or `broker_identity_unavailable`. A second variant
+    exhausts the budget and must report `shared_runtime_unavailable`. A third
+    sends a **malformed** frame instead of closing and must report
+    `protocol_violation` with no retry.
+20. **Sharing policy divergence.** A broker started under `max_leases = 1` and
+    `linger_seconds = 0`; a second client configured `max_leases = 16` and
+    `linger_seconds = 300`. The second client must be refused
+    `shared_runtime_lease_limit`, the refusal must carry
+    `effective_max_leases = 1` **and** `configured_max_leases = 16` with the
+    broker pid and start time, `runtime status` must print both
+    `sharing.effective` and `sharing.configured`, and the runtime must be torn
+    down at the effective linger of 0 — not held for 300 seconds.
+21. **Stale build after an in-place upgrade.** Start a broker, then `rename` a
+    byte-different binary over the same executable path, then attach a new
+    client resolved to that path. The client must refuse
+    `broker_build_identity_mismatch`. The test must also assert that **gate 3
+    passes** in this scenario, so the evidence records that gate 3b, not gate 3,
+    is what refused — the exact claim probe P5 established and revision 1 got
+    wrong.
+22a. **Second broker against a serving incumbent loses the election.** With a
+    broker serving, launch a second `agents-infra runtime broker` under `setsid`
+    with a genuine key. It must exit `EXIT_ELECTION_LOST` at B2 and the test must
+    assert it reached **neither** the port preflight nor the bind: no additional
+    port bind attempt is observed, the incumbent's rendezvous socket `dev`/`ino`
+    is byte-identical before and after, the incumbent's lock is still held, and
+    its leases survive.
+
+    Revision 3's test 22 is withdrawn here, and the reason is worth keeping:
+    it started the second broker "holding a legitimately inherited and held lock
+    descriptor" and expected it to reach preflight and bind. Revision 3 already
+    inherited no descriptor and already lost the election first, so the test
+    asserted a path that no longer existed and could not bind B15's no-unlink
+    rule to anything. Reproduced at the syscall layer as probe P20.A.
+22b. **Contender between the stale-inode cleanup and the bind.** Drive the
+    production broker to the window between B6 (where a stale rendezvous inode
+    may be unlinked) and B15 (where the socket is bound), and have a same-uid
+    process bind the rendezvous path in that window. The broker must exit
+    `broker_rendezvous_bind_conflict`, and the assertion must be that the
+    contender's socket **`dev`/`ino` is unchanged** — not merely that a socket
+    exists at the path, since an unlink-and-rebind would satisfy that too. The
+    control is the same window with the path free, which must bind and serve.
+    Reproduced with its control as probe P20.B/P20.C.
+23. **Wedged peer broker.** A broker takes `broker.lock` and never binds. A
+    second client must refuse **`shared_runtime_peer_start_timeout`**, not
+    `broker_start_timeout`, and must have left no runtime behind. The distinct
+    code is the assertion: a client whose every broker lost the election must
+    never report a failure it did not cause.
+24. **Unrecorded live runtime is unreachable.** The direct regression for the
+    second review's finding F1. Instrument the broker to reproduce revision 2's
+    ordering (authorize, then publish), `SIGKILL` it in the window, and assert
+    that the resulting state — runtime alive, record absent, lock free —
+    **fails** the suite. Then run the production ordering through the same window
+    and assert it passes. A fix without a reproduction of the defect is not
+    evidence; probe P12.A is that reproduction at the mechanism layer.
+24a. **The same state, reached through the successor's own publication.** The
+    revision-4 counterpart, and the regression that stops the fix for review
+    finding F3 from reintroducing finding F1. With a predecessor's runtime alive
+    and named by the record, instrument the broker so B4 publishes its
+    elected-owner record **without** carrying the predecessor's runtime block
+    forward, and `SIGKILL` it before B6. Assert the suite **fails**: the
+    predecessor's runtime is alive and the only record present names no runtime.
+    Then run the production B4 through the same window and assert the successor
+    reclaims. Probe P17.A is the reproduction, P17.B the fix and P17.C the
+    control that reclamation does not simply kill whatever it finds.
+25. **Zombie runtime pid.** Leave the recorded runtime pid as an exited, unreaped
+    process. B6 must read `p_stat == SZOMB`, treat the record as stale, and start
+    exactly one fresh runtime. A build that decides liveness from
+    `kern.proc.pid` alone must fail this test by refusing to start (probe P15).
+26. **Unidentifiable orphan.** A record whose runtime pid is live with a
+    byte-equal start time but whose argv matches neither recorded shape must
+    produce `shared_runtime_orphan_unidentifiable`: nothing signalled, nothing
+    started. The control — the unmutated record — must reclaim, so the refusal
+    discriminates (probe P13.B/P13.D).
+27. **`stop --force` against an unreachable live broker.** Remove the rendezvous
+    socket under a live broker. (a) `stop` without `--force` ⇒
+    `shared_runtime_broker_unreachable_use_force`, nothing signalled. (b)
+    `stop --force` with a record whose broker start time has been altered ⇒
+    `broker_stop_identity_mismatch`, and the live broker must still be running
+    afterwards. (c) `stop --force` with the true record ⇒ the broker is stopped,
+    the lock released, the runtime reclaimed, the endpoint free. (d) With no
+    record and a free lock ⇒ reports `absent` and exits `0`. (e) With no record
+    and a **held** lock ⇒ `shared_runtime_owner_unidentifiable`, nothing
+    signalled (probe P14).
+28. **Divergent broker-start timeouts.** Two clients whose
+    `broker_start_timeout_seconds` differ by a factor of four, both at their
+    schema minimum or above, arriving across a deterministic startup window. Both
+    must obtain leases. The test must assert that the less patient client's
+    deadline exceeded the observed startup, which is what the section 4
+    constraint guarantees, and that when a peer start genuinely does time out the
+    refusal carries both the configured and the effective value.
+
+29. **A client's deadline must not end a broker serving a peer.** The direct
+    regression for review finding F2, and it must be deterministic rather than
+    incidental. Construct the exact ordering: client A forks a broker; A's attach
+    attempts are held off until after its own deadline; the broker completes B15;
+    an independent client B with its own `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` attaches and takes a
+    lease; only then does A evaluate step 4d. Assert that A refuses
+    `broker_start_timeout` with `broker.state = unknown`, that A sent **no**
+    signal to any process, that the broker is still alive, that B's lease is
+    still held, and that the runtime pid is unchanged. The narrowing mutant is
+    "restore the revision-3 live-child inference and `SIGTERM` the broker child",
+    which must redden this test by revoking B's lease. Reproduced at the
+    mechanism layer as probe P19.A (negative) and P19.B (fix), with P19.C
+    recording that A's `wait4(WNOHANG)` answer is identical in both runs — the
+    proxy signal cannot discriminate, which is why the fix is a deletion.
+30. **`runtime-launch` cannot be driven without its own composition.** The direct
+    regression for review finding F1, at the real entry point. (a) Control: a
+    matching `--profile-project`/`--profile`/`--runtime-key` triple with a valid
+    frame ⇒ the process `execve`s and `kern.procargs2` on the **same pid** shows
+    `runtime.executable` and the exact `runtime.argv`. (b) A project directory
+    that composes to a different `runtime_key` ⇒ `runtime_launch_identity_mismatch`,
+    and a poll of `kern.procargs2` until the pid is gone must show it **never**
+    carried the runtime's exec path. (c) An unreadable or non-composing project
+    ⇒ `runtime_launch_identity_unresolvable`, no `execve`, and the test must pair
+    it with the mutant that falls back to a last-known target, which **must**
+    exec — otherwise (c) is satisfied by a launcher that never runs anything.
+    (d) A frame whose `exec_plan_digest` differs from the launcher's own
+    recomputation ⇒ `runtime_authorization_mismatch`, no `execve`. (e) Missing
+    `--profile-project` or `--profile` ⇒ `runtime_launch_identity_unresolvable`,
+    no `execve`. Probes P18.A-E.
+
+30a. **The authorization frame's field set is closed, and every member is a
+    gate.** The direct regression for review `RUN-260825-668303` finding F1, at
+    the real entry point. Drive `agents-infra runtime runtime-launch` with a
+    matching `--profile-project`/`--profile`/`--runtime-key` triple and a frame
+    that is valid in every field **except one**, once per field. Five rows —
+    `schema`, `protocol_version`, `launcher_pid`, `runtime_key`,
+    `exec_plan_digest` — each of which must exit `runtime_authorization_mismatch`
+    carrying `mismatch_field` equal to **that** field, and each of which must be
+    paired with a poll of `kern.procargs2` until the pid is gone proving it
+    **never** carried `runtime.executable`.
+
+    The sixth row of the same table is the discriminating control: nothing bent,
+    which must `execve`. Without it every refusal row is satisfied by a launcher
+    that refuses everything.
+
+    The `protocol_version` row is the one the review demonstrated and it carries
+    two mutants rather than one, because a delete-only mutant proves the check
+    exists and says nothing about the class it covers:
+    - **delete** the comparison — the revision-4 probe verbatim — which must make
+      the wrong-version row reach `execve`;
+    - **narrow** the comparison to `frame.protocol_version >= 1`, which must
+      *also* make the wrong-version row reach `execve` while still refusing
+      version `0`, so the mutant is a narrowing and the surviving gate is proved
+      to be an equality.
+
+    The `schema` row carries the mutant that accepts any schema string. The whole
+    table carries the mutant that refuses without populating `mismatch_field`,
+    which must redden every row's field assertion while leaving every row's exit
+    code green — that contrast is what the field name is for.
+
+    Probes P21.A-D, with the revision-4 reviewer attack rerun verbatim in shape
+    against the corrected launcher as the paired refusal-and-control pair.
+
+    **This test is about values, and it cannot establish closure.** Every row
+    varies one field of a well-shaped frame; a launcher that satisfies all six
+    rows may still be discarding members it never saw. Closure is 12.2.30c's
+    subject, and the split is deliberate — review `RUN-260825-a8a4ef` defeated a
+    launcher that passed every row here.
