+
+    **Revision 8 stops answering that with more rows.** Three revisions have now
+    demonstrated that a table of frames-and-expected-verdicts cannot bind a rule
+    stated as a class, because the table's author has to name the admitted class
+    in advance and the reviewer only has to find one they did not name. What
+    follows replaces the naming with a decision procedure.
+
+    **P23.A — the oracle differential.** The gate's decision is one predicate
+    over one input, and §6.2 B12 step 4 now says so: `M == the allowed five, once
+    each`. That predicate is small enough to implement twice. The test therefore
+    carries an **independent oracle** — a second decision procedure that reads
+    the *generator's own record of what it built*, never the frame bytes and
+    never the decoder — and requires the decoder to agree with it on **every**
+    frame of a generated corpus, on three things: accept versus refuse, the
+    refusal `reason`, and the named member.
+
+    No expected outcome is written next to a frame. That is the entire point: a
+    narrowed gate is caught because it **disagrees with the oracle**, not because
+    someone predicted the class it admits. The two `RUN-260825-c71188`
+    narrowings are killed here by generated frames, not by rows written to answer
+    them.
+
+    The corpus sweeps the decision's degrees of freedom rather than a list of
+    attacks. The dimensions are derived from the predicate's inputs, and each one
+    is where at least one review or revision has actually been wrong:
+
+    | Dimension | What it sweeps | Why it exists |
+    | --- | --- | --- |
+    | occurrence | every allowed member at counts 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 8, 13; several members repeated at once; the whole object repeated | `RUN-260825-c71188`'s arity finding — count 1 is the **accepted** shape and is generated too, so the corpus is not a refuse-everything set |
+    | identity | every allowed name upper-cased, title-cased, suffixed, prefixed, truncated, space-padded, NUL-suffixed, dash-substituted, doubled, and homoglyphed — each carrying **that member's own valid value** | revision 7's measured lesson: an attack a different gate happens to stop is not evidence for the gate under test |
+    | length | unknown names at 0, 1, 2, 3 and every power-of-two boundary from 8 to 1024 with each boundary ±1 | `RUN-260825-c71188`'s length finding; the cutoff `N` is not knowable in advance, so the ladder runs past any plausible one and includes the zero-length name |
+    | position | a repeat adjacent to its original and separated from it, for every member; an unknown member first, middle and last | every duplicate frame this document had ever minted was *separated*, so "refuse only a separated repeat" was green on all of them |
+    | encoding | each allowed name written with a `\u` escape, on the **accepted** side and as a duplicate of its plain form; an unknown name written escaped | the decoded-name rule in B12 step 4; a gate deciding on the wire form fails in **both** directions and only the accepted side can show one of them |
+    | structural | arrays, scalars, `null`, empty input, `{}`, truncation, two objects, trailing garbage, trailing and leading whitespace, pretty-printing | the single-object clause, with the whitespace and pretty-printed rows on the accepted side |
+    | random | seeded, reproducible: unknown names over the printable byte space, and random combinations of drop / repeat / add-unknown / shuffle — including combinations that violate nothing | a hand-built corpus covers what its author enumerated; the seeded tail does not |
+
+    The corpus must contain **both** accepted and refused frames and the test must
+    fail if either count is zero, because a gate proved only by refusals is
+    satisfied by one that refuses everything.
+
+    **P23.B — every mutant is killed by the corpus.** The mutant table is no
+    longer the coverage claim. It is the harness's own calibration, and the
+    obligation inverts: for each mutant, the test finds the generated frames on
+    which it disagrees with the oracle, **reports which frame killed it**, and
+    **fails on a mutant that no generated frame kills** — a coverage hole
+    reported by the harness rather than discovered by the next reviewer. Both
+    disagreement directions count and both are reported: a mutant that *admits*
+    something the oracle refuses, and one that *refuses* something the oracle
+    admits.
+
+    The table must additionally carry mutants that are **blind to every row this
+    document has ever written**, and the test must fail if none is exercised,
+    since a calibration made only of mutants the rows already caught shows
+    nothing about generalising past them. Which mutants those are is **measured,
+    not declared** — see §12.3.1 rule 3 and P23.D. Measurement puts four in that
+    class (non-ASCII names, the zero-length name, and the wire form in each
+    direction); the two mutants review `RUN-260825-c71188` inspired are blind to
+    the revision-6 and revision-7 rows but caught by that review's own, and the
+    adjacency mutant is caught by a revision-7 row.
+
+    Each mutant must additionally be **valid**: it must still admit the plain
+    valid frame, and it must disagree with the oracle only on the side its row
+    declares. Review `RUN-260825-86b7d5` showed why — a mutant that refuses
+    everything satisfies the kill condition with witnesses that are all
+    ordinary accepted frames. §12.3.1 is normative for all three obligations and
+    for the `reject_all_probe` negative that proves they can fail.
+
+    **P23.C — the production entry.** P23.A and P23.B decide at the decoder.
+    P23.C is what binds that decoder to the shipped path, and it carries review
+    `RUN-260825-c71188`'s two exact bypass frames plus one frame per blind
+    mutant. Every row runs twice and in both directions: the specified
+    launcher must refuse it with the named `reason` and member while a poll of
+    `kern.procargs2` shows the pid **never** carried `runtime.executable`, and
+    the narrowed mutant that admits it must reach `execve` — so no row is
+    satisfiable by a launcher that refuses everything. Each row additionally
+    asserts that **the layer-1 decoder's verdict on those exact bytes predicts
+    the production outcome**, which is what makes the two layers one gate rather
+    than two coincidences. The all-valid control must still `execve` — and,
+    since revision 9, the plain valid frame must still `execve` **under every
+    mutant individually** (P23.E). The single all-valid control runs only the
+    specified launcher, which is exactly why it did not discriminate a broken
+    mutant.
+
+    One assertion in P23.C is degenerate and is labelled so rather than counted:
+    `mismatch_field` is omitted when empty, so the zero-length-member row's field
+    expectation cannot distinguish "named the empty member" from "named nothing".
+    That row is carried by its `reason`, by the never-carried-the-target poll,
+    and by its mutant reproducing the bypass.
+
+    **What P23 does not prove.** A generated corpus is a subset of an infinite
+    input space. A narrowing whose admitted class is disjoint from every
+    dimension swept survives P23 exactly as `count == 2` survived revision 7, and
+    revision 8 does not claim otherwise — the corpus bounds the sample, not the
+    space. What bounds the space is structural and lives in §6.2 B12 step 4 and
+    §12.4: the decision must be an equality against a compiled constant set
+    rather than a predicate over anything derived from the frame, and the
+    production launcher must record the multiset it decided on. The two halves
+    answer different halves of the question and neither is evidence alone.
+
+    **The named regressions are kept, and are now regressions rather than the
+    proof.** They are cheap, they are what each review actually minted, and a
+    suite that drops them stops being able to say the defect stayed closed:
+
+    | Regression | Origin |
+    | --- | --- |
+    | Five valid members plus an unknown sixth; `protocol_version` twice, wrong-then-valid and valid-then-wrong; an allowed member omitted; a second object appended (probes P22.B–E) | revision 6, review `RUN-260825-a8a4ef` |
+    | All five members duplicated with their **own valid value**, one row each; four unknown-name near-miss classes (probes P22.G, P22.H) | revision 7, review `RUN-260825-9d5cff` |
+    | Both of the above rerun at the production entry point against the specified decoder and against the narrowed mutant that admits each (probes P22.F, P22.I) | reviews `RUN-260825-a8a4ef`, `RUN-260825-9d5cff` |
+    | Three and five occurrences of an allowed member; a 33-byte and a 1024-byte unknown name (probe P23.C) | review `RUN-260825-c71188` |
+
+    Two facts measured while building this evidence are normative rather than
+    incidental, and both are recorded because each invalidated a row as first
+    drafted:
+
+    - **A value decoder may fold case when it matches object keys to its own
+      fields.** Go's `encoding/json` matches an exact tag first and a case-folded
+      name second, so a member named `Schema` is not discarded the way
+      `future_extension` is — it is absorbed into `schema` and overwrites it.
+      Two consequences: the shape gate must run on the member names **as they
+      appeared in the bytes**, never on whatever a subsequent structure decode
+      resolved them to; and a case-variant attack carrying a *wrong* value is
+      stopped by step 5's equality comparison and therefore proves nothing about
+      step 4. Only a case variant carrying the member's **own valid value**
+      reaches step 5 with everything equal. Every identity-dimension frame in the
+      P23 corpus therefore carries the derived member's source value.
+    - **Both duplicate orders must refuse, and neither order alone is evidence.**
+      A last-wins decoder — Go's `encoding/json` among them — admits
+      `{"protocol_version": 999, …valid…}` and refuses the reverse, because
+      last-wins hands the *valid* value to step 5 in the first case and `999` in
+      the second. A first-wins decoder admits precisely the other one. A suite
+      that tested a single order would show a green "duplicate keys are refused"
+      against a launcher with no duplicate rule at all, on either decoder. What
+      step 4 buys is that the launcher's verdict no longer depends on which dedup
+      rule its value decoder happens to implement.
+
+    The **absent-member clause is a reporting rule, not a bypass gate**, and is
+    specified as such. Step 5 already refuses an absent member, because a missing
+    field decodes to a zero value and the zero value is not the launcher's
+    compiled constant, so deleting the `frame_missing_field` clause admits
+    nothing at the production entry — it only degrades a precise refusal into a
+    mismatch on a zero value. It is here so that the equality in step 4 is one
+    rule rather than two half-rules. P23.A is nevertheless a real gate on it,
+    because the oracle asserts the `reason` and not only the verdict; the
+    corresponding `execve`-level row is recorded as non-discriminating rather
+    than dressed up as a bypass, since a table that quietly drops its
+    non-discriminating rows reads as broader coverage than it has.
+
+    The whole-gate deletion mutant is revision 5's launcher verbatim, and it must
+    reproduce every reviewer defeat this document has recorded. The discriminating
