+### 12.3 Narrowing mutants
+
+Each mutant must redden a **named** test; the mutant list is part of the
+deliverable evidence, and a mutant that reddens nothing is a coverage gap to be
+closed before handoff.
+
+**For the shape gate, revision 8 changes what this table is.** Through revision
+7 the mutant table was the coverage claim: each mutant named a clause, and the
+rows that reddened it were the proof of that clause's class. Three reviews
+defeated that arrangement, most recently by building two mutants nobody had
+listed. Since revision 8 the shape-gate mutants below are the **calibration** of
+probe P23's oracle differential, not the claim. The obligation runs the other
+way: each one must be killed **by the generated corpus**, the harness must report
+which generated frame killed it, and a mutant that no generated frame kills fails
+the suite as a coverage hole. Some of them are deliberately **blind** to the rows
+earlier revisions wrote, and the suite must fail if none is exercised, because a
+calibration made only of mutants the rows already caught says nothing about
+generalising past them.
+
+**Revision 9 makes the mutants themselves gates.** Review `RUN-260825-86b7d5`
+found that revision 8's kill condition — *the mutant disagrees with the oracle on
+at least one frame* — is satisfied by a mutant that refuses **every** frame, and
+that one revision-8 mutant was exactly that: `unknown_by_wire_form` was wired
+through a predicate that discarded the member's wire spelling, so its 48
+"witnesses" were all ordinary **valid** frames. Its `blind` label was a boolean
+in a table that nothing executed.
+
+The general rule, and it is the one worth carrying past this document: *a
+mutation harness's controls are gates, and the evidence rule applies to them
+exactly as it applies to the launcher.* "The mutant was killed" is a positive
+result about the harness. §12.3.1 is what makes it a checked one.
+
+#### 12.3.1 Mutant validity obligations (normative)
+
+Every shape-gate mutant in the table below **must** satisfy all three, and the
+suite **must** fail when one does not:
+
+1. **It must still admit the plain valid frame.** Each mutant narrows or deletes
+   a *refusal* clause, and no such change can turn away a frame the specification
+   admits — including the wire-form mutant, whose whole defect is on the escaped
+   spelling and not on the plain one. A mutant that refuses the plain valid frame
+   is not a narrowing; it is broken, and every kill credited to it is satisfied
+   by a launcher that refuses everything. Checked at the decoder (P23.B) **and**
+   at the production `runtime-launch` entry point, one process per mutant
+   (P23.E). The production check is not optional: it is where a reviewer
+   reproduces it.
+2. **It must declare the side it disagrees on, and disagree only there.** A
+   mutant either **admits** frames the specification refuses or **over-refuses**
+   frames it admits. The measured witness set must be non-empty on the declared
+   side and empty on the other (P23.B). Exactly one mutant in the table is an
+   over-refuser; every other is an admitter. Without this rule a mutant that has
+   silently become the opposite defect still reports a large kill count.
+3. **Blindness is measured, never declared.** Each mutant names the hand-written
+   **baselines** it is green on — `rev6` (the rows revision 6 wrote for review
+   `RUN-260825-a8a4ef`), `rev7` (the rows revision 7 wrote for review
+   `RUN-260825-9d5cff`), and `review_c71188` (the two frames that review minted)
+   — and the harness recomputes the truth from an inventory of those rows,
+   failing on an **over-claim and on an under-claim alike** (P23.D). Green on a
+   baseline means: for every row in it, the mutant's decoder verdict — accept,
+   reason **and** named member — is identical to the specified decoder's. That is
+   strictly stronger than "the row's test still passes", and stronger in the safe
+   direction: decoder agreement on a row implies the whole downstream launcher
+   path agrees on it. Each baseline must contain more than its valid control, or
+   blindness on it would be vacuous. A mutant blind to any baseline must also
+   report a **corpus-only** killing frame, identified by bytes as absent from the
+   inventory.
+
+These three obligations are themselves proved by a negative. `reject_all_probe`
+is not a gate mutant: it is review `RUN-260825-86b7d5`'s defect reproduced — a
+membership predicate that refuses everything — and the suite must show all three
+rules reddening on it (P23.F). Without that, §12.3.1 is positive-path-only
+evidence about the harness.
+
+**Measured correction to revision 8, and it is the point of rule 3.** Revision 8
+claimed seven mutants blind to *every* hand-written row. Measurement says four:
+`unknown_ascii_only`, `unknown_nonempty_only`, `dup_keyed_on_wire_form` and
+`unknown_by_wire_form`. The claim was wrong in both directions.
+`dup_only_exactly_two_total` and `unknown_allow_over_32` are blind to `rev6` and
+`rev7` but **not** to `review_c71188` — those rows are precisely what caught
+them, which is why the mutants exist. And `dup_only_when_separated` is caught by
+one `rev7` row nobody had noticed: the same-value duplicate rows append the
+repeat at the end of the object, and `exec_plan_digest` is the **last** member in
+the frame's field order, so that one row alone places the repeat *adjacent* to
+its original. Ten mutants are blind to some baseline but not all; four are caught
+by a row that already existed.
+
+**No behaviour changed and `protocol_version` stays 6 for a fourth revision.**
+The repair is one line inside the mutant-selection wiring, and §9's bump rule
+depends on whether the *specified* decoder's admitted set moved. That is measured
+rather than argued: revision 8's wiring is retained under the name
+`rev8_unknown_wiring` and must agree with revision 9's on the whole generated
+corpus and every hand-written row — verdict, reason and named member — over 417
+frames (P23.F).
