+### 12.4 Wiring proof
+
+A test must assert that the **production** `RunPi` path invokes the attestation
+chain — for example by driving the real entry point against an instrumented
+broker that records which gates were exercised, and failing if any gate in
+section 7.2 was not evaluated. A gate that exists but is never called from
+production promises nothing.
+
+Revision 3 adds three entry points with gates of their own, and each needs the
+same proof, because a gate reachable only from a unit test is not a gate:
+
+- the **broker** must be shown to evaluate B1 (session leader) and then B2
+  (election) on the real `agents-infra runtime broker` path, in that order and
+  before any record read, record write, reclamation, port preflight, runtime
+  creation or bind. The instrumented run must fail if a broker that lost either
+  gate is observed performing any of those six — the record **read** and **write**
+  are in that list only since revision 4, because B3 and B4 now precede
+  everything else and a broker that failed the detachment gate after the election
+  would have rewritten a predecessor's record on its way out;
+- the **launcher** must be shown to evaluate its descriptor-3 gate, its own
+  profile recomposition, and its frame gates on the real
+  `agents-infra runtime runtime-launch` path, **in that order**, and the test must
+  fail if `execve` is reached on any path other than a valid frame naming its own
+  pid and carrying the digest the launcher itself computed. Naming the production
+  call site is mandatory here rather than optional: review finding F1 was
+  precisely that the mechanism worked in a probe whose launcher received its
+  target through an environment channel with no normative counterpart, so a test
+  that supplies the target from outside the composition proves nothing about this
+  entry point.
+
+  Revision 5 adds a second obligation to the same clause, from the second review
+  finding at this entry point: the instrumented run must record **which frame
+  fields the launcher compared**, and must fail if any field B11 transmits was
+  read without being compared, or if any field was compared by something other
+  than equality. Coverage of the five gates is not inferable from a green suite —
+  revision 4's suite was green while `protocol_version` was parsed and dropped —
+  so this is an assertion about the set of comparisons performed, not about the
+  outcomes of the ones that happen to be exercised.
+
+  **Revision 6 makes that obligation answerable, and it is the reason the
+  revision exists.** Revision 5's clause asked the instrumented run to fail if a
+  transmitted field went uncompared — but it specified the compared set as the
+  launcher's five struct members, and a launcher that decodes into five members
+  has no way to report a sixth. The obligation was therefore satisfiable by an
+  implementation that could not have detected the violation it was asked to
+  report. Revision 6 restates it on the **decoded key multiset**: the instrumented
+  run must record the member names the decoder observed, *duplicates retained*,
+  and must fail unless that multiset and the compared set are both exactly the
+  five allowed keys once each. Duplicates must be retained in the record for the
+  same reason they must be refused — a deduplicating record reports precisely the
+  shape the forged frame is trying to present. **Revision 7 adds the same
+  requirement one axis over: the names must be recorded as they appeared in the
+  bytes**, not as a structure decode resolved them, because a value decoder that
+  folds case reports `Schema` as `schema` and the record would then describe a
+  frame that was never sent. The evidence must be written before the verdict is
+  acted on, so a refusing run leaves the same record a passing one does.
+
+  **Revision 8 adds the obligation that bounds what a corpus cannot**, and it is
+  the half of §12.2.30c's claim that does not live in a test. Probe P23 sweeps
+  the decision's dimensions and kills every mutant it can reach; a narrowing
+  whose admitted class is disjoint from every dimension swept survives it, and
+  three reviews in a row have found exactly such a narrowing. So the wiring proof
+  must also establish the **shape** of the decision, not only its outcomes:
+
+  - the recorded evidence must show that the launcher decided by comparing the
+    decoded multiset `M` against a **compiled constant set** — the same constant
+    B11 writes the frame from — and not by evaluating any predicate over a
+    quantity derived from the frame. An occurrence count compared to a literal, a
+    name length compared to a ceiling, a position or adjacency test, a
+    case-folded or prefix match, or a comparison against the bytes of a key are
+    each a violation of §6.2 B12 step 4 whether or not any test input reaches
+    them;
+  - the same evidence must show that the decoder P23.A and P23.B decide against
+    is the decoder the production `runtime-launch` path calls. P23.C asserts the
+    cross-layer agreement on every frame it drives — the layer-1 verdict must
+    predict the production outcome — but agreement on a subset is not identity,
+    and naming the shared call site is what closes the gap. This is the same
+    obligation, and the same reason for it, as the launcher clause above: a gate
+    reachable only from a unit test is not a gate, and a decision procedure
+    exercised only in a unit test is not the shipped decision procedure;
+- **`stop --force`** must be shown to run the *same* B6 reclamation subroutine as
+  a broker, not a second implementation of it. Assert it by naming the shared
+  call site, and by requiring every section 12.2.26 refusal to reproduce
+  identically through the operator path;
+- the **client's step 4d** must be shown to reach its refusal without any
+  `kill`/`signal` call on the production path. Assert it by instrumenting the
+  signal-sending seam the client uses for its own Pi group and failing if it is
+  invoked with a broker pid, not by reading the code — review finding F2 was a
+  live call site, not a missing one;
+- **`status`** must be shown to evaluate the `broker.lock` probe on the real
+  `agents-infra runtime status` path before it prints `absent`. A `status` that
+  reports `absent` without having attempted the lock cannot distinguish
+  `starting-unverified`, and that distinction is the whole of finding F3's
+  remedy.
+
+The ordering claims of section 6.4 must likewise be asserted against production
+rather than against a model: the test drives the real broker, `SIGKILL`s it at
+each named instant, and checks the corresponding row. Revision 4 adds two
+instants to that list — B2-B4 and B4-B6 — and the second is the one that regresses
+silently, because a broker whose B4 drops the predecessor's runtime block behaves
+correctly in every row except the one where a predecessor's runtime is alive.
+
+A closing note on what the probes are and are not. Probes P1-P23 model these
+mechanisms at the **syscall layer**, in a separate module, against stand-in
+processes. They are evidence that each mechanism behaves as specified on this
+platform, and — for P7, P12.A, P17.A, P18.C, P19.A, P21.B/E/F, P22's six decoder
+deletions and five narrowings, P23's eighteen calibrated mutants of which four
+are measured blind to every hand-written row, and P23.F's `reject_all_probe` —
+that the defect being fixed was real.
+They are **not** evidence that the shipped `agents-infra` calls them. This section
+is what makes that link, and `TASK-260825-lsojra` owes it.
+
+The revision-8 evidence module is packaged so that link can actually be checked
+without reconstruction. Review `RUN-260825-c71188` finding F2 was that revision
+7's sources were attached as loose files referencing a helper harness no attached
+resource defined, so the reviewer's first rerun failed to compile and the harness
+had to be rebuilt by hand. Revision 8 attaches one archive containing `go.mod`,
+`go.sum`, the vendored dependency, the `TestMain` helper dispatcher, the identity
+helper, the launcher, the decoder and all three probe suites, and it was verified
+by extracting it into an empty directory and running it with `GOPROXY=off`. The
+same defect exists one revision earlier and is recorded here rather than left to
+be rediscovered: the attached revision-4 sources call `notifySignal`, which is
+defined in no attached revision-4 resource, so the revision-4 rerun below is a
+reconstruction and is labelled as one.
+
+Two revision-4 probe claims are **superseded rather than repeated**, because the
+revision-4 review's finding was as much about what a probe may claim as about
+what the launcher must compare:
+
+- P18.D asserted an exit code that four gate-4 branches share, so "the record's
+  plan and the launcher's own composition must agree" named more than it
+  exercised. 12.2.30a's `exec_plan_digest` row, asserting `mismatch_field`,
+  is what carries that claim now (probe P21.D).
+- P18.E's log read "foreign pid, truncated frame and absent FIFO all refuse
+  without exec", but its absent-FIFO case asserted `runtime_launch_unauthorized`
+  — the same code the EOF branch produces — so it did not establish that the
+  descriptor gate ran at all. 12.2.18b and 12.2.30b, asserting the refusal
+  `reason`, carry it now (probes P21.E, P21.F).
+
+A third correction is to the probe harness rather than to a claim, and it is
+recorded because it is the kind of defect that makes green evidence worthless.
+Revision 4's P18 recorded the exec paths a pid ever carried into a 64-slot
+channel with a non-blocking send, so once sixty-four observations accumulated,
+further ones were dropped — including, potentially, the single observation that
+the launcher had `execve`d the target. Every "never carried the exec path"
+assertion in P18 was therefore satisfiable by a full buffer. It did not bite in
+revision 4 because every refusing launcher there exited in milliseconds; it would
+have bitten P21.E and P21.F, whose refusals are deliberately slow. Revision 5's
+harness records into a mutex-guarded set with no capacity bound.
+
+## 13. Acceptance proof
+
+`TASK-260825-1lc8o7` must record, from a real run against the deployed
+`mlx_lm.server` profile with `linger_seconds = 0`:
+
+1. **Two independent tracked spawns.** Two `task-board spawn ... --background`
+   invocations returning two distinct `RUN-…` handles. Captured from
+   `task-board spawn status` for each RUN.
+2. **Independent process ancestry.** For each run, the `agents-infra pi` client
+   pid, its pgid, its ppid, and its session id. The two clients must have
+   distinct pgids and must not share a parent shell pid. A single-shell
+   `pi & pi` demonstration is explicitly **not** acceptance.
+3. **Two Pi processes.** Two distinct Pi pids, each a child of its own client,
+   each with a distinct `PI_CODING_AGENT_SESSION_DIR` under its own
+   `runs/<run_key>/`.
+4. **One attested Qwen runtime.** Exactly one `mlx_lm.server` pid bound to
+   `127.0.0.1:18011`, with its `kern.proc.pid` start time proving it predates
+   the second client's start, and its argv byte-equal to the configured
+   `[executable] + runtime.argv`. Both leases must report the same runtime pid.
+   The record must also show that this pid's start time is byte-equal to the
+   `runtime.start_time` the broker published **before** the process became
+   `mlx_lm.server` (section 6.2, B10): that equality is what proves the durable
+   record was written ahead of the runtime rather than after it, and it is
+   readable directly from `broker-state.json` and `broker.log`.
+
+   The recorded `runtime.post_exec.argv` and `runtime.exec_plan_digest` must also
+   be shown to match what the **launcher** independently composed, read from
+   `runtime.log`. Under revision 4 the broker never tells the launcher what to
+   run (section 6.2, B12); the run does not prove item 4 unless the record shows
+   both processes reached the same plan from the same project directory.
+5. **The second spawn crossed the startup window.** The second spawn must be
+   issued while the first client's broker is still starting, and the record must
+   show it: the second client's start stamp precedes the runtime's `/v1/models`
+   first-ready stamp from `broker.log`, and the second client's own log shows at
+   least one broker child that exited `EXIT_ELECTION_LOST` before it attached.
+   That exit status is the direct, kernel-delivered evidence that this client
+   offered a broker and a peer's broker already owned the runtime; revision 2
+   asked for an `EWOULDBLOCK` observation the client no longer makes, because it
+   never touches `broker.lock`. A 27B MLX
+   model takes tens of seconds to become ready, so this is the ordinary case, not
+   a contrived one — but if the two spawns happen not to overlap the window, the
+   run does **not** count as proof of items 4 and 6 and must be repeated. This is the
+   acceptance-level counterpart of test 12.1.2: revision 1 of this specification
+   would have passed items 4 and 6 whenever the window was missed, and failed
+   `shared_runtime_peer_start_timeout` whenever it was hit.
+6. **Overlap.** Per-run start and end wall-clock stamps whose intervals
+   intersect, plus one `agents-infra runtime status --json` snapshot taken
+   inside the intersection showing `lease_count = 2` with both RUN ids.
+7. **Independent success.** Both runs produce their own successful output and
+   their own exit status; neither run's output appears in the other's session
+   directory.
+8. **Neither run's client signalled the broker.** Both clients' logs must show
+   no signal sent to any broker pid, and the `agents-infra runtime status`
+   snapshot from item 6 must show the broker's pid unchanged from the one that
+   first appeared in `broker-state.json`. If either spawn happened to hit its
+   `broker_start_timeout`, its refusal must report `broker.state = unknown` and
+   the other run's lease must be intact in the next status snapshot. This is the
+   acceptance-level counterpart of test 12.2.29: under revision 3 the second
+   spawn's timeout would have terminated the first spawn's broker.
+9. **Bounded cleanup after both terminal states.** After both runs reach a
+   terminal state: no listener on `127.0.0.1:18011`, no `mlx_lm.server` process
+   matching the recorded pid, the rendezvous socket unlinked, `leases/` empty,
+   `broker-state.json` removed, and `broker.lock` acquirable. Each assertion
+   recorded with the command that produced it.
+
+   The cleanup must be attributed to the **effective** `linger_seconds`, not to
+   the value the proving client configured: capture
+   `agents-infra runtime status --json` inside the overlap window and record
+   `sharing.effective.linger_seconds = 0` together with the broker pid that
+   fixed it (section 5.4). Without that reading, a teardown observed after the
+   final release is consistent with a peer having started the broker under a
+   nonzero linger and the runtime having died for an unrelated reason.
+
+Evidence must be sanitized: pids, start times, paths, hashes, and exit codes
+only. No model output beyond a success marker, no environment values, no keys.
+
+## 14. Rejected alternatives
+
+| Alternative | Reason rejected |
+| --- | --- |
+| First client spawns a detached runtime and later clients attach to the bare listener | Nobody is the runtime's parent, so nothing can `wait()` it, liveness degrades to pid polling, and the direct-child invariant that the whole cycle-8 contract rests on is lost |
+| launchd/systemd user service owning the runtime | Moves lifetime and identity outside agents-infra, requires per-host installation, and makes "stop after the final lease" an external policy we cannot prove |
+| File-based leases with heartbeat timeouts as the release mechanism | A timer cannot distinguish a dead client from a slow one; connection teardown is exact and free |
+| Revoke a lease when its heartbeat expires | Kills live agents mid-request for a false positive; process death is already detected exactly |
+| Key the broker by project | Two projects with byte-identical profiles would deadlock on the same port, which is the exact failure this story removes |
+| Put the socket at the full-key path | Verified to exceed `sun_path` at 186 bytes; refusal is the only honest outcome without a truncated address |
+| Treat the truncated rendezvous name as identity | Truncation is an address; the full key, digest, and endpoint are verified over the connection |
+| Environment override for socket path, mode, or timeouts | An override is a bypass around every gate in section 7 |
+| Trust the broker's announced runtime facts without independent verification | Transitive trust; the client's own `sysctl` reads cost microseconds and remove it |
+| Ask the runtime to attest itself over HTTP | Self-minted evidence from an executable that is explicitly out of the threat model |
+| Claim listener ownership from preflight + liveness + `/v1/models` | False under the post-preflight bind race; cycle 8 already rejected this claim |
+| Implement socket-ownership attestation now | Requires cgo or a raw `proc_info` syscall to close only an already-accepted residual risk; recorded as future hardening with a reserved refusal code |
+| Default `mode = "shared"` | Would silently change behavior for all 121 existing project configurations |
+| Invent a `max_leases` default | Capacity on a single-accelerator host is an operator decision; the existing schema requires fields rather than guessing them |
+| Nonzero default `linger_seconds` | Leaves a runtime alive with zero leases, contradicting the story AC and weakening the cleanup proof |
+| Share one Pi `agent/` directory across concurrent clients | Concurrent writers to Pi settings/trust/auth is a lost update, not isolation |
+| Expose the operator surface as `agents-infra pi runtime …` | The Pi wrapper forwards non-flag tokens to the model as message operands |
+| Let clients signal the runtime process group in shared mode | A client does not own the runtime; one client's cleanup would break every peer |
+| Acquire `broker.lock` with a blocking `LOCK_EX` and a deadline (revision 1) | The broker holds the lock for its whole serving life, so a waiter blocks until the deadline while a healthy broker serves — probe P7 reproduces the failure. On darwin the waiter cannot even `close` the descriptor afterwards, because `close` blocks behind the pending `flock` |
+| A second `broker_ready_timeout_seconds` knob for non-starters | `broker_start_timeout_seconds >= runtime.startup_timeout_seconds` already bounds a waiter correctly; a second knob adds a second thing to misconfigure and no behaviour. The two outcomes are separated by refusal code instead |
+| Hand the broker an already-held, inherited `broker.lock` descriptor (revision 2) | The holder relationship is not observable. `flock` can prove only that *some* open file description holds the lock, so during a peer's startup a descriptor open on the correct inode but unlocked passes the gate while the runtime port and rendezvous path are both still free — the second review's F2, reproduced as probe P8.D's precondition. Revision 3 has the broker acquire the lock itself, which makes the question disappear rather than answering it |
+| Elect the starter client-side and let the broker inherit the election | Same defect one step earlier: whatever the broker inherits, it must infer something about a process that may already be gone. Moving the election into the broker costs one `flock` per losing candidate, which probe P8.A measures as a process that exits before any side effect |
+| Add a starter→broker capability pipe so the broker can verify who forked it | It proves who its parent was, not that the lock is held now, and it fails spuriously whenever the starter dies first. Both properties the composition needed are supplied directly by having the broker hold the lock itself |
+| Spawn the runtime and publish its identity afterwards (revision 2) | A `SIGKILL` in between leaves a live runtime, a free lock, and no record — reproduced end to end by probe P12.A, together with reclamation's `no-record` verdict while that runtime is still serving |
+| Publish a placeholder record before the fork and fill in the pid afterwards | The pid does not exist before the fork, so the unrecorded window merely moves rather than closing. Publish-before-run closes it by making the process **unable to become the runtime** until the record naming it is durable |
+| Adopt an orphan runtime instead of killing it | No live process is its parent, so nothing can `wait()` it and the direct-child invariant cannot be restored. It is killed and replaced, never attached to |
+| Give the launcher a shared secret so the authorization frame is unforgeable | Same-uid forgery is already out of the threat model, and a secret on disk is a new secret to protect for no gain. The frame's job is to refuse an accidental invocation and, above all, to observe a broker that is no longer there — pid equality and EOF decide both |
+| Decide liveness from `kern.proc.pid` alone | It succeeds for an exited, unreaped process (probe P15). One zombie would be read as a live runtime and would block every future start |
+| Let `stop --force` signal a broker identified by pid alone | Probe P14.A: a record whose start time no longer matches must leave the live process untouched. `stop --force` verifies the same tuple a broker does, and reclaims through the very same subroutine |
+| Refuse `agents-infra runtime broker` outright as an ad-hoc command | Revision 2 tried, and the mechanism it used was the one the second review broke. A `setsid` invocation with a genuine recomputed key is the same binary serving the same profile; what must be refused — a different binary, a repointed key, a divergent profile, a second broker — is refused by gate 3, the key recomputation, gate 7 and the B2 election, each on its own evidence |
+| Verify the inherited lock by re-locking descriptor 3 (revision 1) | `flock` is per open file description, so it always succeeds and the gate admits every ad-hoc invocation — probe P1.A. The working mechanism is a second descriptor (P1.B) with the unheld control (P1.C) |
+| `fcntl` `F_GETLK` to inspect the inherited lock | POSIX record locks cannot observe a BSD `flock`; the reviewer's probe returned `F_UNLCK` against a held file |
+| Double-fork the broker so it reparents to init immediately | Terminal independence already comes from `setsid` (probe P6). Keeping the broker a real child is what lets the starter detect a startup failure with `wait4(WNOHANG)` and report `broker_start_failed` at once instead of after the full timeout |
+| `--profile-source PATH` naming a single config file | A profile composed across more than one config file cannot be reproduced from one path, and the broker's self-verification is fatal on mismatch, so every composed shared launch would die. The broker re-runs the full composition from `--profile-project DIR` |
+| Refuse a client whose sharing policy differs from the broker's | Sharing policy changes no correctness property and is deliberately outside `profile_digest`; refusing would re-split runtimes the digest deliberately merged. The starter's policy governs and the divergence is reported instead |
+| Let a later client's `linger_seconds` or `max_leases` re-tune a running broker | Policy would depend on arrival order, and `runtime status` could never report a stable effective value |
+| Carry the executable, argv, cwd and timeout in the authorization frame (the obvious fix for review finding F1) | It works, and it makes the pipe a **command channel**: whoever holds the write end then chooses what runs, rather than only whether it runs. Recomposition keeps *what* pinned to configuration both processes read independently, bound by `runtime_key` equality over a digest that already covers executable, argc, every argv token and both runtime timeouts. The cost is one extra composition; the gain is that there is no plan on the wire to forge or truncate |
+| Pass the launcher a single composed-profile file instead of `--profile-project DIR` | The same defect revision 2's `--profile-source PATH` had, one process later: a profile composed across several files cannot be reproduced from one path, and the launcher's self-verification is fatal on mismatch, so every composed shared launch would die at B12 |
+| Let the launcher fall back to a last-known target when composition fails | A failed read is not an absence. Probe P18.C's mutant does exactly this and **does** exec, which is the whole reason the refusal is a real distinction rather than a launcher that never runs anything |
+| Keep the client's step-4d `SIGTERM` but check the rendezvous socket first to see whether the broker is serving | A `connect()` before the signal is a TOCTOU window, not a check: the broker can bind and grant a lease between the probe and the `kill`. Revision 4 does not narrow the window; it removes the authority, because nothing the client needs depends on having it (probe P19.D shows the first-lease grace already covers the case the signal pretended to) |
+| Give the client a startup-only cancellation capability, disabled when the broker starts serving | This is a second single-flight problem with no lock to hand: the capability must be revoked atomically at exactly the `serving` transition, observed by a different process. Revisions 2 and 3 both failed on precisely that class of cross-process inference, and there is nothing left to cancel — every broker startup step is already independently bounded |
+| Double-fork so the broker is not the client's child, removing the temptation to signal it | It removes the exit status the client uses to tell `EXIT_ELECTION_LOST` from a startup failure, and buys nothing revision 4 needs: the signal is already gone |
+| Declare the elected-owner window impossible (revision 3) | It is reachable — probe P16.A produces it with one `SIGSTOP` — and a false impossibility claim is worse than a named limitation, because it stops anyone from handling the case at all. Revision 4 narrows the window to one atomic write, names the state `starting-unverified`, and bounds the operator path |
+| Have `stop --force` signal the single candidate holder when process enumeration finds exactly one | Probe P16.D produces two candidates identical on uid, binary inode, argv and `p_stat` while exactly one holds the lock. `flock` cannot name its holder on this platform, so "exactly one candidate" is a property of the moment, not of the system. An operator with `lsof` can decide; agents-infra reports and stops there |
+| Write the owner identity **into** `broker.lock` so acquisition and identification are one operation | The identity would still be written after the `flock` returns, so the window moves rather than closing; and a `linkat`-based exclusive create that *is* atomic with its content gives up the property the whole design rests on — the kernel releasing the lock when the holder dies, with no timer and no timeout |
+| Publish the elected-owner record at B4 without the predecessor's runtime block | Reintroduces the second review's finding F1 through the door the third review's F3 fix opened: a `SIGKILL` between B4 and B6 would leave a predecessor's live runtime that the only present record does not name. Probe P17.A reproduces it |
+
+## 15. Scope boundaries
+
+Out of scope for this story, stated so no implementation infers them:
+
+- Windows and any non-POSIX platform. `pi_platform_windows.go` keeps refusing
+  managed Pi launch; sharing adds no Windows surface.
+- Request routing, queuing, fairness, batching, or admission control inside the
+  runtime. The broker hands out an endpoint; it never proxies a request.
+- Model acquisition, conversion, quantization, benchmarking, or DFlash
+  verification. Unchanged from cycle 8.
+- Cross-host or network sharing. The endpoint stays `127.0.0.1` and the
+  rendezvous stays a filesystem socket.
+- Migrating existing project configurations to `mode = "shared"`. That is an
+  operator action; `TASK-260825-1lc8o7` opts in exactly one profile for the
+  acceptance proof.
+
